\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Asian Summary; Joint G-7 Deal Helps Japan Weaken Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,935 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mostly higher, led by the Tokyo market after the G-7 agreed to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the JPY's recent surge as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  struggles with multiple natural disasters and an ongoing nuclear emergency. The Nikkei is +2.6%, the S&P/ASX is +1.6%, the HSI is +0.6%, the Kospi is +1.3%, the Taiex is +1.3%, the Shanghai Composite is +0.7% and the STI is +0.3%; the Sensex is 1.0% lower on political concerns after leaked cables. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the JPY pulled back sharply after the G-7 pledged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  dumped yen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Japan's MOF sold JPY through the BOJ for the first time since September and Japanese finance minister Yoshihiko Noda said each G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its own timezone. The USD/JPY jumped to 81.88 from 78.93 late Thursday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 115.18 from 110.62 and the EUR/USD is at 1.4070 from 1.4020. China needs to effectively lower the massive amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  it buys to rein in inflation, a professor at PBOC's training institute says. February prices of newly built homes in 68 of the 70 Chinese cities covered by National Bureau of Statistics survey rose on-year. Mounting worries over Japan's nuclear crisis will prompt India to revisit its own nuclear safety strategies, says Indian Prime Minister Manmohan Singh. Spot gold is at $1,410.40/oz, up $6.40 from the NY close. April Nymex crude oil futures are up $1.40 at $103.26/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,410.50/oz, up $6.50 since the New York close, helped by hopes the Japanese nuclear crisis may be resolved without a significant further escape of radiation, which is encouraging more gains in equities. "There's a feeling that by early next week this (nuclear emergency) might be behind us," says Darren Heathcote, head of trading at Investec in Sydney. "As with any of these stock market sell-offs in recent years, gold suffered through margin calls, but that has turned around and it is trading up with equities," he says. Gold is also getting bids as oil rises on the likelihood of U.N.-backed military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya. "That's another factor helping. Libya is back in focus," he says. Gold has strong support at $1,380/oz, its 100-day moving average and next technical resistance is likely at $1,424, the November 2010 high. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 June futures are up 1.9% at 9100, off the intraday high of 9200, on a sharply weaker yen following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coordination with the G-7. Still, some investors are rushing to lock in profits ahead of Japan's three-day weekend, capping gains, says Investrust CEO Hiroyuki Fukunaga, adding that futures may trade in a 8,500-9,500 range with high volatility next week. Investors are waiting to see if the problems at Japan's troubled nuclear power plants wind down and how much damage Japanese firms have incurred because of the recent earthquake. "Unless these points become clear, investors cannot help but take a conservative stance even if they think the Nikkei will rise in the long-run." (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0550 GMT [Dow Jones] India's RBI is likely to hike its repo rate by 25 bps when it next meets on May 3, as inflation is likely to remain at elevated levels for a longer period, says CLSA in a report. The house attributes its higher inflation view to higher global commodity prices, and policymakers' reluctance to dramatically slow aggregate demand. "Consequently, there will also be a greater propensity to favour higher subsidies and more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a higher pass through," the house says. It estimates India's inflation in March 2012 at 8.0%, and thinks RBI will be expecting inflation then at 6.0%.(sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are up 0.7% at 1009.28 at the midday break, tracking gains in most regional bourses; trade is thin. Sluggish trading volume "reflects investors remain uncertain over what will happen during the weekend," says \uc2 Thanachart Securities\uc2  Senior Vice President Pichai Lertsupongkit; he adds, heavyweight energy plays are buoyant on rising oil prices. Investors are keeping eyes on the development of Japan's nuclear crisis and on any reaction from Col. Moammar Gadhafi after the U.N. approves a no-fly zone and military force in Libya. He tips resistance at 1010-1015, and support at 1000. Among the most active shares, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) is up 2.0% at THB50.25, IRPC (IRPC.TH) is up 0.8% at THB6.05, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) is up 0.6% at THB182, Banpu (BANPU.TH) rises 0.8% to THB744 and PTT (PTT.TH) is up 1.2% at THB340. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0540 GMT [Dow Jones] Korean treasury bonds are mostly lower, with the yield curve getting steeper after recent flattening, participants say; the three-year yield is quoted down 1 bp at 3.65%, while the five-year yield is up three bps at 4.08%, and the ten-year yield up six bps at 4.47%. "Longer-end bonds had strengthened too much recently, with concerns over the global economic recovery," boosting the appetite for those bonds, says a bond manager with a securities firm, who expects the yield curve to steepen a bit more until early next week ahead of the government auction for ten-year and twenty year bonds coming up later this month. He tips a 102.90-103.20 range for June bond futures for the session; they are up five ticks at 103.13 on bargain hunting. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 0.7% at 22,440.96 on a modest rebound after falling 1.8% Thursday, supported by news that the G-7 agreed to coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to staunch the yen's recent surge. "Although we believe this nuclear incident (in Japan) would not trigger a credit crisis in Asia and the recent global equities selloff was primarily as a result of panic selling, we view the equity market would remain volatile as the nuclear situation develops in Japan," says Core Pacific-Yamaichi. It expects the wind power and equipment sector as well as coal counters to continue to outperform, given their current undemanding valuations and China's suspension of nuclear power projects. It adds, the current trough valuations of China developers should bottom out following recent tightening measures in first-tier cities and developers' imminent price cuts. Among China developers, China Overseas (0688.HK) rises 5.4% to HK$13.96, while \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) adds 5.5% to HK$12.74. Volume is modest at HK$40.38 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] The Japanese earthquake and tsunami currently are likely to have only a small impact on the NZ economy, with NZ's GDP likely to be cut by 0.02%, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Philip Borkin; "Beyond the near-term impact, there is some room for NZ to benefit once rebuilding activity takes place through its supply of forestry products." Japan is NZ's fourth largest trading partner in terms of both merchandise trade and 4% of visitors to NZ last year came from Japan. Borkin adds, the impact would need to be reviewed if the nuclear crisis impacts production and consumption patterns more than expected or if oil prices were to rise again. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] India's interbank call rate is lower but still remains at elevated levels as banks scramble to meet a cash shortfall after companies make heavy withdrawals to pay their advance taxes, says a dealer with a state-run bank. The overnight call rate is at 7.60% vs 7.75% at the last close. The collateralized borrowing and lending obligation rate is at 6.70% from 6.75% at open. "We expect the rates will come down next week on some government spending and expected increase in deposits with the banks," the dealer says. He tips the call rate in a 7.40%-7.80% range in the near term. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0454 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered Bank maintains its flattener recommendation on the 2-year/5-year OIS spread (initiated on January 10 at 58bps; target 30bps; stop-loss 78bps). The house says large rate increases appear unlikely if supply-side dynamics continue to exert upward pressure on headline inflation. Also, elevated market rates (the 1-year CD rate has increased 340bps since April 2010) amid tight liquidity have already dampened investment sentiment. The house tips another 50 basis points of rate hikes for the rest of 2011 spread equally over next two meetings. The house expects economic growth to slow to 8.1% in FY12 from 8.5% in FY11. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones]Singapore's STI is up 0.2% at 2950.06 midday, rebounding after its 0.9% fall Thursday amid gains for regional markets on hopes Japan's nuclear catastrophe will be resolved. Volume is light at 531 million shares, reflecting pre-weekend caution. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says following the recent pull-back, its Singapore universe is trading at 13.4X 2011E, vs its five-year average of 14.5X. "Our P/B versus ROE analysis implies an end-2011E MSCI Singapore Index target of 438, implying 25% potential upside (corresponding STI target is 3711, also +25%)." The house continues to be positive on industrials for their exposure to global trade, and says its key stock ideas include Keppel (BN4.SG), \uc2 SembCorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) and NOL (N03.SG). It also likes Yangzijiang (BS6.SG), "one of the cheapest shipbuilding stocks globally" and is positive on commodity and tourism plays, like Olam (O32.SG), Wilmar (F34.SG), Noble (N21.SG), Genting (G13.SG) and CDLHT (J85.SG). Hutchison Port (NS8U.SG) debuts after lunch, with analysts expecting a tepid performance. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com) [ 03-18-11 0157ET ]\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are up 0.5% at 3499.949 in moderate volume, off an intraday high of 3511.673, with gains in most commodity-related stocks keeping the main index in positive territory, traders say. They expect the main index to move in a 3490-3520 range for the rest of the session. "Overall, the market is consolidating ahead of the weekend and as concerns over the situation in Japan and the Middle East linger," says a trader with Lautandhana Securities. Coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) is up 3.3% at IDR3,100, rival Indika (INDY.JK) is up 1.3% at IDR3,900, while \uc2 Indo Tambangraya\uc2  (ITMG.JK) is also up 2.1% at IDR47,350 on hopes for strong 2010 earnings. Profit-taking hits Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK), which is down 1.5% at IDR4,975.(edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 03-18-11 0157ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
