\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei +3.1% On Weaker Yen, Fall In Radiation Levels\b0\par\par\uc2 1,786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0445 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is up 3.1% at 9239.65 on the weaker yen following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (USD/JPY now 81.63), declines in radiation levels at Japan's troubled nuclear plants, and stronger share prices in most other Asian markets, says Hiroichi Nishi, equity division general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Radiation levels at the afflicted plant have declined slightly since Thursday afternoon, according to Japan's Nuclear and Industrial Safety Agency. "If buying in Nikkei futures kicks in, the Nikkei cash index may rise to 9,300 later today," Nishi says. 33/33 Topix subindexes are higher with exporters leading; Kyocera (6971.TO) is up 5.0% at Y7,830 and Fanuc (6954.TO) is up 2.3% at Y11,640. \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  (6502.TO), a major nuclear power plant maker, is up 6.3% at Y356. \uc2 Daikin Industries\uc2  (6367.TO) is up 8.8% at Y2,400 on expectations for demand when the earthquake recovery process begins.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 ends up 0.3% at 3339.51 as offshore markets improve and sentiment rises, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Suzanne Kinnaird; "The market has been weaker, while the leads offshore are positive and that tends to reflect on our market." She adds that Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) ended up 9.4% at NZ$2.22 after Thursday announcing better than expected 1H results; "Possibly it was market sentiment Thursday that meant we didn't see more of a reaction Thursday." She adds weakness in the NZD/USD was helping exporters; Skellerup (SKL.NZ) ended up 6.4% at NZ$1.33, Cavalier (CAV.NZ) ended up 5.9% at NZ$3.60 and Rakon (RAK.NZ) ended up 1.9% at NZ$1.06. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.3% at 1496.73 at midday after gains on Wall Street Thursday and amid mostly stronger regional markets. "After losses over the past few days, it's not a surprise to see this rebound ... but with uncertainties in Japan and the Middle East, we think the gains are precarious," says a dealer, adding the market may succumb to some profit taking in the afternoon session, ahead of the weekend. He tips 1500 as immediate resistance and 1489, the intraday low, as support. Market breadth is positive, with 408 advancers against 199 decliners. Gainers include \uc2 Tenaga Nasional\uc2  (5347.KU) is up 1.0% at MYR6.08, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) is up 1.1% at MYR5.63, Kulim (2003.KU) is 3.0% higher at MYR3.43 and Kossan Rubber (7153.KU) is up 1.9% at MYR3.25. Decliners include Maybank (1155.KU), down 0.5% at MYR8.71, and \uc2 Telekom Malaysia\uc2  (4863.KU), down 1.0% at MYR3.90. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] India bond yields are likely to rise in the near-term as India will begin its borrowing program for 1H of FY12 from April, says a dealer at a primary dealership. The Indian government is aiming to raise between 58%-64% of its borrowing for the next fiscal year during the April-September period, a senior finance ministry official said Friday. "The range is mostly in line with market expectations. But yields will rise from these levels when bond auctions begin," the dealer says. He expects the 8.08% 2022 bond yields to touch 8.20% when auctions begin from 8.11% currently. The government borrowing calendar is expected on March 25. The government typically frontloads its borrowing program. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  keeps Underweight on the India market as it believes while near-term fund flows may keep driving the country's outperformance, the economic momentum should stay weak, and will likely continue to pressure market multiples. It says further rate hikes look like a given following RBI's 25 bps hike in key policy rates Thursday; "inflation concerns remained RBIs focus even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  felt that investment growth may be slowing down," the house says. It adds "in our view, higher domestic fuel prices and borrowing costs are likely to be constraints on domestic growth and we maintain our view for GDP growth (forecast +7.7% in FY11) to be at the low-end of the consensus expectation." The Sensex is down 0.6% at 18,050.50. (raghavendra.upadhyaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end up 0.1% at 461.08, off an intraday low of 455.92, with investors coming back in the last minutes to buy after the key index fell more than 7.0% between Monday and Thursday, though there is an absence of any fresh market-boosting news. "Local sentiment has risen after foreigners were active in buying select blue-chip stocks, including Vietinbank (CTG.VH), after they were net sellers during the past few days," says a trader with EuroCapital Securities. He predicts more gains next week due to two factors: positive 1Q earnings and a positive end to Japan's nuclear crisis. Among the most actives, Vietinbank ended up 5.0% at VND31,700, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) closed up 4.6% at VND25,000 and \uc2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \uc2 finished 2.2% lower at VND44,500. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, snapping a 5-session losing streak, following Wall Street's advance overnight. The main gauge PSEi is up 0.6% at 3829.88 with all sub-indices registering gains. "Global equities are staging a technical rally after the big economies said they will help with the recovery efforts in Japan," says Lawrence De Leon, a trader with Accord Capital Equities. De Leon adds, the index may trade in a 3750-3900 band next week. Most active stocks include Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 1.9% at PHP6.29; PLDT (TEL.PH), up 1.2% at PHP2,038; \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH), off 2.2% at PHP28.60. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are lower as traders cut their positions on concerns of further rate hikes as the RBI says it will continue with its anti-inflationary stance, says a trader with state-run bank. The 8.08% 2022 bond is at 99.76 from 99.99 at Thursday's close. He adds, "globally investors are pulling out of safe haven assets like bonds as fear over Japan's nuclear crisis is easing." He says bond prices are also pressured ahead of government's borrowing calendar for the first half of the next FY (due on March 25). A government official on Friday says, about 58%-64% of the fiscal year's borrowing will be during the April-September period. The trader tips the 8.08% 2022 bond yield in a 8.08%-8.14% band for the session from 8.08% at Thursday's close; the yield is at 8.11%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Non-Japanese G-7 central banks may hold their fire against the JPY Friday if the USD/JPY remains well supported after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiated JPY-selling in the morning, says Satoshi Okagawa, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corporation\uc2 ; but the pledge of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means that they would be highly likely to jump in Monday, a national holiday in Japan, if the USD/JPY falls then, he says. "With Japan on holiday Monday, it could be easy for people to try to push the dollar-yen down again, and any falls would likely bring the other G-7 countries into the market." Okagawa adds that any renewed falls after Asian hours Friday "could also bring the ECB or other central banks in." The USD/JPY is at 81.80. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0415 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is off 0.2% at 18,106.52 after opening in positive territory, and is underpeforming Asian markets as rising crude prices and fears of political instability are making investors nervous, says a local analyst. Thursday, a leaked U.S. diplomatic cable suggested India's ruling Congress party offered bribes to win a crucial vote in Parliament on the U.S.-India nuclear deal in 2008; "every few weeks there is some fresh corruption news and the market is worried about the cumulative impact of these incidences," the analyst says. Nymex April Crude is at $103.01/ bbl, up 159 cents. Maruti Suzuki (532500.BY) is +1.9% at INR1,191.35 as the JPY's sharp pull-back against the USD allays some concerns about the cost of imports from Japan. \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  (532454.BY) is +1.6% at INR323.40 on media reports that the firm is likely to buy \uc2 Qualcomm\uc2 's (QCOM) India broadband wireless licenses. Among losers, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) is down 1.7% at INR1,013.70. The Sensex is tipped in a 17,950-18,200 range.(sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0411 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR is lower as firm local stocks and the EUR's strength vs the greenback support the local unit, says a trader with a privately-run bank. The pair is at 45.08 vs 45.19 in late Asian trade Thursday. "The pair may find support at INR45. Oil importers are likely to buy near 45.00-45.05," he says. The G-7 finance ministers agreed to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the JPY and he reckons that could impact the positioning of major world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Sensex is up 0.4%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 0.8% at 22,451.29 midday, on a rebound after falling 1.8% Thursday, supported by news the Group of Seven industrialized nations agreed to a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to staunch the yen's recent surge, says Daniel So, analyst of Sun Hung Kai Financial. He expects the index to remain in a 22,000-22,500 range in the near-term. "Trading sentiment remains cautious as investors remain concerned over the nuclear crisis in Japan," he adds. Those with strong corporate results are outperforming, with \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) rising 2.9% to HK$10.76, while Henderson Land (0012.HK) adding 3.7% to HK$47.30. Volume is modest at HK$39.21 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 03-18-11 0045ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
