\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Philippine Shares End +0.6%;3750-3900 Tipped Next Wk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, snapping a 5-session losing streak, following Wall Street's advance overnight. The main gauge PSEi is up 0.6% at 3829.88 with all sub-indices registering gains. "Global equities are staging a technical rally after the big economies said they will help with the recovery efforts in Japan," says Lawrence De Leon, a trader with Accord Capital Equities. De Leon adds, the index may trade in a 3750-3900 band next week. Most active stocks include Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 1.9% at PHP6.29; PLDT (TEL.PH), up 1.2% at PHP2,038; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aboitiz Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AP.PH), off 2.2% at PHP28.60. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are lower as traders cut their positions on concerns of further rate hikes as the RBI says it will continue with its anti-inflationary stance, says a trader with state-run bank. The 8.08% 2022 bond is at 99.76 from 99.99 at Thursday's close. He adds, "globally investors are pulling out of safe haven assets like bonds as fear over Japan's nuclear crisis is easing." He says bond prices are also pressured ahead of government's borrowing calendar for the first half of the next FY (due on March 25). A government official on Friday says, about 58%-64% of the fiscal year's borrowing will be during the April-September period. The trader tips the 8.08% 2022 bond yield in a 8.08%-8.14% band for the session from 8.08% at Thursday's close; the yield is at 8.11%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Non-Japanese G-7 central banks may hold their fire against the JPY Friday if the USD/JPY remains well supported after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiated JPY-selling in the morning, says Satoshi Okagawa, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corporation\uc2 ; but the pledge of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means that they would be highly likely to jump in Monday, a national holiday in Japan, if the USD/JPY falls then, he says. "With Japan on holiday Monday, it could be easy for people to try to push the dollar-yen down again, and any falls would likely bring the other G-7 countries into the market." Okagawa adds that any renewed falls after Asian hours Friday "could also bring the ECB or other central banks in." The USD/JPY is at 81.80. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0415 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is off 0.2% at 18,106.52 after opening in positive territory, and is underpeforming Asian markets as rising crude prices and fears of political instability are making investors nervous, says a local analyst. Thursday, a leaked U.S. diplomatic cable suggested India's ruling Congress party offered bribes to win a crucial vote in Parliament on the U.S.-India nuclear deal in 2008; "every few weeks there is some fresh corruption news and the market is worried about the cumulative impact of these incidences," the analyst says. Nymex April Crude is at $103.01/ bbl, up 159 cents. Maruti Suzuki (532500.BY) is +1.9% at INR1,191.35 as the JPY's sharp pull-back against the USD allays some concerns about the cost of imports from Japan. \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  (532454.BY) is +1.6% at INR323.40 on media reports that the firm is likely to buy \uc2 Qualcomm\uc2 's (QCOM) India broadband wireless licenses. Among losers, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) is down 1.7% at INR1,013.70. The Sensex is tipped in a 17,950-18,200 range.(sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0411 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR is lower as firm local stocks and the EUR's strength vs the greenback support the local unit, says a trader with a privately-run bank. The pair is at 45.08 vs 45.19 in late Asian trade Thursday. "The pair may find support at INR45. Oil importers are likely to buy near 45.00-45.05," he says. The G-7 finance ministers agreed to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the JPY and he reckons that could impact the positioning of major world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Sensex is up 0.4%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 0.8% at 22,451.29 midday, on a rebound after falling 1.8% Thursday, supported by news the Group of Seven industrialized nations agreed to a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to staunch the yen's recent surge, says Daniel So, analyst of Sun Hung Kai Financial. He expects the index to remain in a 22,000-22,500 range in the near-term. "Trading sentiment remains cautious as investors remain concerned over the nuclear crisis in Japan," he adds. Those with strong corporate results are outperforming, with \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) rising 2.9% to HK$10.76, while Henderson Land (0012.HK) adding 3.7% to HK$47.30. Volume is modest at HK$39.21 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is consolidating after gaining following the G-7's pledge of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the JPY, says \uc2 HiFX\uc2  senior trader Stuart Ive. "The market will now be a bit more controlled and the Kiwi and Aussie will be underpinned by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The pair is at 0.7301 vs 0.7179 early this morning. Ive adds that overnight the market will continue to focus on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and what this means for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which may see further rises in the NZD. The ongoing turmoil in Libya is also a focus. Ive expects short-term support to remain at 0.7180 with resistance at 0.7370. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] Credit Agricole expects Hong Kong's jobless rate to fall to 3.2% by year-end given the buoyant economic activity. "However, it may be difficult for the jobless rate to fall meaningfully in the coming two to three months as people tend to switch between jobs following bonus time," says senior strategist Frances Cheung. Hong Kong December-Februry jobless rate fell to 3.6% from 3.8% in November-January. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher midday, tracking firmer regional markets, led by bargain hunting in brokerages and gains in coal miners. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.6% at 2913.59; analysts peg immediate resistance at 2950. "The upside is likely to be limited as investors remain cautious on China's possible tightening moves during the weekend and Japan's ongoing radiation leaks," says Li Lei, an analyst from Gold State Securities. Among leading gainers, brokerage Citic Securities (600030.SH) rises 2.1% to CNY14.19, and GF Securities (000776.SZ) adds 2.8% to CNY41.75. Coal miners advance on hopes of higher coal prices on the back of stronger oil prices; Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) is up 2.4% at CNY32.08, while \uc2 China Coal Energy\uc2  (601898.SH) is 0.8% higher at CNY11.30. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.5% higher at 1291.27. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds are lower as local stocks rebound (the Taiex is +1.3%) after their recent losses, says a local trader. But he notes trading volume is thin; "there are still uncertainties about Japan's nuclear crisis, so most traders are cautious and they are not trading actively," he adds. The yield of the most-traded 10-year government bonds rises to 1.3399% vs 1.3172% in late Asian trade Thursday; he tips the 10-year yield to trade in a 1.3000%-1.3800% range next week. "If Japan could somehow resolve its nuclear crisis, the 10-year yield is likely to rise to 1.3800%," he says. The 5-year yield is at 1.0600% vs 1.0438% late Thursday. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY remains lower midday at 6.5713 from 6.5744 late Thursday; a Shanghai-based European bank trader tips it to trade in a 6.5650-6.5750 range for the rest of the session. "The PBOC is unlikely to let the yuan move much amid panic overseas. We are not expecting a rate hike either this weekend for the same reason." The escalating crisis in Japan linked to the earthquake and radiation leaks at a nuclear plant, and the political turmoil in the Middle East have roiled markets worldwide this week. The central parity is set lower at 6.5668 from 6.5690 Thursday. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4480/6.4530 from 6.4600/6.4640 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW extends losses to 1,126.50 from 1,128.50 earlier, compared with 1,135.30 late Thursday in Seoul as the Kospi rises 1.3%, while a bout of stop-loss sell orders on the JPY/KRW earlier after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also boosted the won, traders say, tipping support for the USD/KRW at 1,125. Fund-repatriation related to dividend payouts to foreign investors provided some support for the USD/KRW earlier, but the pair is now headed further down on the Kospi's strength, says a local bank trader. But he adds, offshore players seem rather quiet now after putting in a round of stop-loss USD-sell orders earlier. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 03-18-11 0027ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\pic