escalating crisis in Japan linked to the earthquake and radiation leaks at a nuclear plant, and the political turmoil in the Middle East have roiled markets worldwide this week. The central parity is set lower at 6.5668 from 6.5690 Thursday. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4480/6.4530 from 6.4600/6.4640 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW extends losses to 1,126.50 from 1,128.50 earlier, compared with 1,135.30 late Thursday in Seoul as the Kospi rises 1.3%, while a bout of stop-loss sell orders on the JPY/KRW earlier after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also boosted the won, traders say, tipping support for the USD/KRW at 1,125. Fund-repatriation related to dividend payouts to foreign investors provided some support for the USD/KRW earlier, but the pair is now headed further down on the Kospi's strength, says a local bank trader. But he adds, offshore players seem rather quiet now after putting in a round of stop-loss USD-sell orders earlier. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is +1.2% at 1982.32 as Japan's nuclear fears persist but panic subsides; "it's a relief for now because there is no more bad news coming from Japan," says \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Cho Byung-hyun. He expects the Kospi to recover its key 2000 mark in the near term and further gains are expected if Japan could fully focus on rebuilding efforts. Steelmakers and oil refiners are higher as investors expect them to benefit from supply shortage following Japan's earthquake; Posco (005490.SE) is up 3.2% at KRW502,000 and \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) is up 2.4% at KRW212,000. But heavyweight tech plays are underperforming the broader market; investors are weighing the impact of Japanese components makers' troubled supply on the overall industry, analysts say. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) is 