600188.SH) is up 2.4% at CNY32.08, while \uc2 China Coal Energy\uc2  (601898.SH) is 0.8% higher at CNY11.30. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.5% higher at 1291.27. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds are lower as local stocks rebound (the Taiex is +1.3%) after their recent losses, says a local trader. But he notes trading volume is thin; "there are still uncertainties about Japan's nuclear crisis, so most traders are cautious and they are not trading actively," he adds. The yield of the most-traded 10-year government bonds rises to 1.3399% vs 1.3172% in late Asian trade Thursday; he tips the 10-year yield to trade in a 1.3000%-1.3800% range next week. "If Japan could somehow resolve its nuclear crisis, the 10-year yield is likely to rise to 1.3800%," he says. The 5-year yield is at 1.0600% vs 1.0438% late Thursday. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY remains lower midday at 6.5713 from 6.5744 late Thursday; a Shanghai-based European bank trader tips it to trade in a 6.5650-6.5750 range for the rest of the session. "The PBOC is unlikely to let the yuan move much amid panic overseas. We are not expecting a rate hike either this weekend for the same reason." The escalating crisis in Japan linked to the earthquake and radiation leaks at a nuclear plant, and the political turmoil in the Middle East have roiled markets worldwide this week. The central parity is set lower at 6.5668 from 6.5690 Thursday. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4480/6.4530 from 6.4600/6.4640 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW extends losses to 1,126.50 from 1,128.50 earlier, compared with 1,135.30 late Thursday in Seoul as the Kospi rises 1.3%, while a bout of stop-loss sell orders on the JPY/KRW earlier after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also boosted the won, traders say, tipping support for the USD/KRW at 1,125. Fund-repatriation related to dividend payouts to foreign investors provided some support for the USD/KRW earlier, but the pair is now headed further down on the Kospi's strength, says a local bank trader. But he adds, offshore players seem rather quiet now after putting in a round of stop-loss USD-sell orders earlier. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is +1.2% at 1982.32 as Japan's nuclear fears persist but panic subsides; "it's a relief for now because there is no more bad news coming from Japan," says \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Cho Byung-hyun. He expects the Kospi to recover its key 2000 mark in the near term and further gains are expected if Japan could fully focus on rebuilding efforts. Steelmakers and oil refiners are higher as investors expect them to benefit from supply shortage following Japan's earthquake; Posco (005490.SE) is up 3.2% at KRW502,000 and \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) is up 2.4% at KRW212,000. But heavyweight tech plays are underperforming the broader market; investors are weighing the impact of Japanese components makers' troubled supply on the overall industry, analysts say. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) is flat at 891,000 and \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) is off 0.4% at KRW28,850. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is up 1.3% at 4615.6 after hitting a fresh 3-day high of 4619.6, as banks, materials and energy rise strongly after G-7 agrees on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and signs of stabilization in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear crisis. This week's price action suggests a strong bottom is in place near the major Fibonacci retracement support at 4469.0, but traders are eyeing the 200-day moving average at 4637.0 for potential resistance, particularly with oil prices shooting up after the U.N. imposed a no-fly zone over Libya. Trading volume is heading well above average levels for a Friday. That's partly attributable to a host of S&P index changes due to take effect at the close. But there's also been a rush of buying to re-establish positions since the market was beaten down excessively this week and Japan's nuclear crisis hasn't deteriorated as much as the market had feared. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 03-18-11 0018ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
