\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/KRW Extends Fall; 1,125 Support -Traders\b0\par\par\uc2 1,726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW extends losses to 1,126.50 from 1,128.50 earlier, compared with 1,135.30 late Thursday in Seoul as the Kospi rises 1.3%, while a bout of stop-loss sell orders on the JPY/KRW earlier after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also boosted the won, traders say, tipping support for the USD/KRW at 1,125. Fund-repatriation related to dividend payouts to foreign investors provided some support for the USD/KRW earlier, but the pair is now headed further down on the Kospi's strength, says a local bank trader. But he adds, offshore players seem rather quiet now after putting in a round of stop-loss USD-sell orders earlier. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is +1.2% at 1982.32 as Japan's nuclear fears persist but panic subsides; "it's a relief for now because there is no more bad news coming from Japan," says \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Cho Byung-hyun. He expects the Kospi to recover its key 2000 mark in the near term and further gains are expected if Japan could fully focus on rebuilding efforts. Steelmakers and oil refiners are higher as investors expect them to benefit from supply shortage following Japan's earthquake; Posco (005490.SE) is up 3.2% at KRW502,000 and \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) is up 2.4% at KRW212,000. But heavyweight tech plays are underperforming the broader market; investors are weighing the impact of Japanese components makers' troubled supply on the overall industry, analysts say. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) is flat at 891,000 and \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) is off 0.4% at KRW28,850. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is up 1.3% at 4615.6 after hitting a fresh 3-day high of 4619.6, as banks, materials and energy rise strongly after G-7 agrees on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and signs of stabilization in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear crisis. This week's price action suggests a strong bottom is in place near the major Fibonacci retracement support at 4469.0, but traders are eyeing the 200-day moving average at 4637.0 for potential resistance, particularly with oil prices shooting up after the U.N. imposed a no-fly zone over Libya. Trading volume is heading well above average levels for a Friday. That's partly attributable to a host of S&P index changes due to take effect at the close. But there's also been a rush of buying to re-establish positions since the market was beaten down excessively this week and Japan's nuclear crisis hasn't deteriorated as much as the market had feared. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are up 0.6% at 1008.52 in moderate trade. The benchmark index rebounds after its recent falls but ongoing concerns over Japan's nuclear reactors still weighs on upside. Renewed tension in the Middle East may boost energy stocks but is not positive for overall sentiment, says DBS Vickers Securities Vice President Arparporn Sawaengpak. She adds profit taking may intensify if the SET Index can't hold 1000; she tips resistance at 1010. Among most actives, IRPC (IRPC.TH) is up 1.7% at THB6.10, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) is up 1.1% at THB183, Banpu (BANPU.TH) is up 0.3% at THB740, \uc2 Bangchak Petroleum\uc2  (BCP.TH) is up 2.7% at THB19 and \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) is up 1.0% at THB49.75. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0328 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are lower in tandem with softer U.S. Treasurys overnight after a three-day rally; trade is sluggish suggesting players want to stay on the sidelines ahead of the weekend. "The market mostly priced in Japan's nuclear situation, so yields are unlikely to drop much from their current levels," says a bond dealer. However, developments on the radiation leakage at Japan's nuclear power plant remain in focus. Yields are expected to stay in range trade for rest of the day. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.30%/3.27% from 3.29%/3.25% in late Asian trade Thursday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.60%/3.50% from 3.55%/3.50% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.77%/3.73% from 3.75%/3.70%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.2% at 459.71 after dipping to 455.92, with selling in select financial blue-chip stocks amid negative sentiment from Japan's earthquake and nuclear failure and concerns over the Libyan strife, which could cause oil prices to rise further. "Local sentiment is weak because there are several factors now, ranging from Japan's complicated situation, a potential Libyan war and high \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs for local companies," says a brokerage trader. Among leading decliners, Masan (MSN.VH) is down 3.1% at VND78,000, \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  Drilling (PVD.VH) is down 2.5% at VND48,200 and Vincom (VIC.VH) is down 1.7% at VND116,700. The trader tips immediate support for the key index at 455. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] Midland Holdings (1200.HK) is up 3.0% at HK$5.93 and is buoyed by its general dividend payout, with a special dividend of 11.8 HK cents/share on top of a handsome final dividend of 20.28 HK cents, which if including its interim dividend of 26.8 HK cents would yield a mouth-watering 10.2%. Still, the stock is unlikely to rally much further from here due to earnings uncertainty this year given the recent decline in property transaction volume; Thomson Reuters consensus currently pegs FY11 earnings to decline to HK$460 million from FY10 net profit of HK$533 million. CS cuts Midland target to HK$7.18 form HK$8.82 on lower EPS forecasts but keeps the stock at Outperform; with 45% cash at the current share price, and 5.6X ex-cash FY11 EPS, "we think Midland is a cheap option to any good response to the upcoming primary launches." (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is likely to open a tad higher tracking advances in Asian markets and may trade in a narrow range today, a local analyst says. "The macro picture is not very encouraging because the RBI Thursday raised its inflation forecast and (Nymex April) crude is back above $100 (a barrel)," the analyst says. He adds, investors will be reluctant to take fresh positions ahead of the weekend given the uncertainty over the situation in Japan and the Middle East. He expects buying in \uc2 Maruti Suzuki India\uc2  (532500.BY) on the JPY's sharp pull-back against the USD after the G-7 agreed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to staunch the JPY's recent surge. The company has a significant JPY exposure and has lost nearly 8.0% over the past week mainly due to concerns about rising costs of imports from Japan. A local dealer tips the Sensex to trade in a 18,000-18,250 range today. The Sensex ended down 1.1% at 18,149.87 Thursday.(sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR is expected to open lower, tracking USD weakness against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says a senior trader with a foreign bank. The pair may open around 45.10-45.15 from 45.19 late Thursday and is tipped in a 45.00-45.20 band. "The pair may find support if importers buy dollars at lower levels." Foreign funds were net sellers of a provisional INR11.29 billion worth of Indian stocks on Thursday; 1-month NDFs are quoting at 45.41/45.51. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are up 0.6% at 3503.356 in moderate volume, helped by gains across global markets and the stronger rupiah, trader say. They expect the main index to top 3520. "Buying in most coal-related stocks on higher oil prices has driven the main index higher amid profit-taking by local funds," says a trader with Valbury Securities. Coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) is up 2.5% at IDR3,075, rival Indika (INDY.JK) is up 1.3% at IDR3,900, while \uc2 Indo Tambangraya\uc2  (ITMG.JK) is also up 1.3% at IDR47,000 on hopes for strong 2010 earnings. Profit-taking hits consumer good producer \uc2 Unilever\uc2  (UNVR.JK) which is down 0.3% at IDR16,050. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] A rough week for the AUD/USD has exposed a near-term low point for the pair, says Sue Trinh, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with RBC in Hong Kong. Weighing on the pair has been the heightened Japan nuclear worries, with the AUD/USD falling to a low of 0.9706 at one point--its lowest level since December. The pair has rallied today on the back of the coordinated G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move to kneecap a strong yen, reaching 0.9929 in recent trade, though it's still off the early year highs of around 1.2000. "We expect AUD/USD's descent is close to exhaustion over a 1-3 month horizon," says Trinh, who also recommends buying AUD/NZD toward 1.35, with a target of 1.40 by 4Q. The pair is last at 1.3647. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is moving lower, tracking gains in the EUR and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against USD. The pair is at 43.73 after settling at a seven-week high of 43.92 late Thursday. "Some banks have pared their long positions and that's why we're seeing relatively heavy volume this early. We have yet to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says a Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. Traders peg USD/PHP support at 43.70 and resistance at 44.00. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2342ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i00030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
