reign bank. The pair may open around 45.10-45.15 from 45.19 late Thursday and is tipped in a 45.00-45.20 band. "The pair may find support if importers buy dollars at lower levels." Foreign funds were net sellers of a provisional INR11.29 billion worth of Indian stocks on Thursday; 1-month NDFs are quoting at 45.41/45.51. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are up 0.6% at 3503.356 in moderate volume, helped by gains across global markets and the stronger rupiah, trader say. They expect the main index to top 3520. "Buying in most coal-related stocks on higher oil prices has driven the main index higher amid profit-taking by local funds," says a trader with Valbury Securities. Coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) is up 2.5% at IDR3,075, rival Indika (INDY.JK) is up 1.3% at IDR3,900, while \uc2 Indo Tambangraya\uc2  (ITMG.JK) is also up 1.3% at IDR47,000 on hopes for strong 2010 earnings. Profit-taking hits consumer good producer \uc2 Unilever\uc2  (UNVR.JK) which is down 0.3% at IDR16,050. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] A rough week for the AUD/USD has exposed a near-term low point for the pair, says Sue Trinh, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with RBC in Hong Kong. Weighing on the pair has been the heightened Japan nuclear worries, with the AUD/USD falling to a low of 0.9706 at one point--its lowest level since December. The pair has rallied today on the back of the coordinated G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move to kneecap a strong yen, reaching 0.9929 in recent trade, though it's still off the early year highs of around 1.2000. "We expect AUD/USD's descent is close to exhaustion over a 1-3 month horizon," says Trinh, who also recommends buying AUD/NZD toward 1.35, with a target of 1.40 by 4Q. The pair is last at 1.3647. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is moving lower, tracking gains in the EUR and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against USD. The pair is at 43.73 after settling at a seven-week high of 43.92 late Thursday. "Some banks have pared their long positions and that's why we're seeing relatively heavy volume this early. We have yet to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says a Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. Traders peg USD/PHP support at 43.70 and resistance at 44.00. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are expected to open slightly lower, tracking overnight weakness in U.S. Treasurys and RBI's anti-inflationary stance, says a fixed income analyst with a bond broking firm. "The liquidity crunch will also pressure bond prices. But major downside is not expected as most of RBI's policy impact was factored in the previous session." He adds all eyes are now on the government's borrowing calendar for the first half of the next FY (due on March 25). He tips the 8.08% 2022 bond yield in a 8.06%-8.10% band for the session, compared to 8.08% at the last close. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0301 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei ends the morning session up 1.8% at 9120.93 after the G-7 decision on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  invention spurred short-covering, but well off its high of 9251.64. Still, traders may become more cautious in the afternoon, as strong concerns remain over the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant's problems ahead of the 3-day weekend in Japan, says Tachibana Securities operating officer Kenichi Hirano. Markets are closed on Monday for spring equinox. Exporters are finding some support on the yen's weakness today, but buying probably won't go beyond short-covering, says Hirano. Japan oil explorers are outperforming the broader indexes on higher crude prices, with \uc2 Japan Petroleum Exploration\uc2  (1662.TO), or "Japex", up 7.4% at Y3,825 and Inpex (1605.TO) up 3.3% at Y561,000. Shares of \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO), or "Tepco", were up 16% at Y922 following massive share price losses this week, briefly going limit-up (Y948) mid-morning. Hirano adds that one sector that may attract more substantial buying interest may be alternative energy such as solar power; Kyocera (6971.TO), which makes solar panels, is up 3.5% at Y7,720. (juro.osawa@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2336ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
