hlight\uc2 \f2  MOF's JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has set off strong JPY-buying flows from non-Japanese investors, say several dealers. "The MOF is still in the market, and that's why the dollar is being stabilized around this level because there are many non-Japanese investors who are buying the yen," one says. Still, the amount of total \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far is likely less than Y1 trillion, they say. "As (finance minister) Noda mentioned, we may see another big wave coming in European trading hours," another dealer adds; Noda earlier mentioned the possibility of the ECB joining the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaign. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] The G-7 joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb sharp JPY gains "is likely to have a meaningful impact in discouraging speculative JPY purchases" for now but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may remain under upward pressure so long as Japan's troubled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors, as well as geopolitical concerns, weigh on investors' willingness to buy higher-yielding, riskier \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "General risk aversion, currently driven by the unstable situation at Japan's damaged nuclear power plant and ongoing tension in the Middle East, tends to strengthen the JPY," Yamamoto says. "In order to ease the upward pressure on the JPY, a resolution of the nuclear plant's problems and an easing of tensions in the Middle East are required." He says the key level to watch on any further USD/JPY rises following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that started earlier is 83.30, the high hit after last Friday's earthquake. The USD/JPY is last at 81.44. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2315ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0002p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
