(finance minister) Noda mentioned, we may see another big wave coming in European trading hours," another dealer adds; Noda earlier mentioned the possibility of the ECB joining the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaign. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] The G-7 joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb sharp JPY gains "is likely to have a meaningful impact in discouraging speculative JPY purchases" for now but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may remain under upward pressure so long as Japan's troubled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors, as well as geopolitical concerns, weigh on investors' willingness to buy higher-yielding, riskier \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "General risk aversion, currently driven by the unstable situation at Japan's damaged nuclear power plant and ongoing tension in the Middle East, tends to strengthen the JPY," Yamamoto says. "In order to ease the upward pressure on the JPY, a resolution of the nuclear plant's problems and an easing of tensions in the Middle East are required." He says the key level to watch on any further USD/JPY rises following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that started earlier is 83.30, the high hit after last Friday's earthquake. The USD/JPY is last at 81.44. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls after a lower central parity is set at 6.5668 from Thursday's 6.5690; the central parity is close to the record low of 6.5651 set Mar. 7. In OTC trade, the USD/CNY is at 6.5708 from 6.5744 late Thursday; a Beijing-based local bank trader tips trade in a 6.5680-6.5780 band. "The fixing was lower than expected. I don't think it's a signal that the PBOC wants to let the yuan appreciate, rather it's to manage the yuan's stability during the Japanese crisis. Starting yesterday, we're seeing a lot of export settlements; that has pushed the dollar higher against the yuan," she says. Offshore, the one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4470/6.4520 from 6.4600/6.4640 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2314ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0002o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
