litical concerns, weigh on investors' willingness to buy higher-yielding, riskier \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "General risk aversion, currently driven by the unstable situation at Japan's damaged nuclear power plant and ongoing tension in the Middle East, tends to strengthen the JPY," Yamamoto says. "In order to ease the upward pressure on the JPY, a resolution of the nuclear plant's problems and an easing of tensions in the Middle East are required." He says the key level to watch on any further USD/JPY rises following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that started earlier is 83.30, the high hit after last Friday's earthquake. The USD/JPY is last at 81.44. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls after a lower central parity is set at 6.5668 from Thursday's 6.5690; the central parity is close to the record low of 6.5651 set Mar. 7. In OTC trade, the USD/CNY is at 6.5708 from 6.5744 late Thursday; a Beijing-based local bank trader tips trade in a 6.5680-6.5780 band. "The fixing was lower than expected. I don't think it's a signal that the PBOC wants to let the yuan appreciate, rather it's to manage the yuan's stability during the Japanese crisis. Starting yesterday, we're seeing a lot of export settlements; that has pushed the dollar higher against the yuan," she says. Offshore, the one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4470/6.4520 from 6.4600/6.4640 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.5% at 458.12, led by selling in select blue-chip stocks on concerns over the global impact of Japan's earthquake and the Libyan strife. Dealers say trading volume is thin because many institutional investors are staying on the sidelines amid rising global uncertainties which could result in disadvantages for the local economy; "one of the key concerns is a possible rise in oil prices, which could hurt our economic recovery," says a Hanoi-based brokerage trader. He adds the fact that foreigners turned net sellers in recent sessions is also hurting local sentiment. Among leading decliners, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bao Viet Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BVH.VH) is down 4.5% at VND74,500, \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroVietnam Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PVF.VH) is down 2.7% at VND21,300 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \f28 \uc2 \f2 is down 1.8% at VND44,700. The trader tips immediate support for the key index at 455. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2313ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0002n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
