\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: LME Metals Mostly Lower On Mild Profit Taking\b0\par\par\uc2 1,634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals are mostly lower in Asia on some mild profit taking after the powerful rally Thursday on a weak USD and Japan reconstruction hopes. The USD has rallied against the yen this morning on G-7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and stabilized against the EUR, so it's no longer giving metals much help. "Those who were buying into weakness on Tuesday are sitting on some nice gains, so you would expect a bit of profit taking," says a Singapore-based trader at a European bank. LME 3-month copper is at $9,523/ton, down $41 with 1,957 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0254 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian CDS are tighter as the market responds favorably to the G7's pledge to jointly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the JPY, while tentative signs of stabilization at Japan's quake-stricken nuclear reactors also provide support. "No news is good news, as so far we don't know what's going on," an Asia-based trader says, of the nuclear situation. The Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment-grade CDS index is at 109.5-110.5 bps vs Thursday's 115 bps. "It wouldn't be surprising if these tighter levels continued," so long as there isn't a serious deterioration in the Japan situation, he adds. Hyva Global's new $375 million 5-year global dollar bond deal, which was priced at par late Thursday to yield 8.625%, is trading around 102.375/102.625; suggesting that investors are "comfortable with the credit and pricing, and the market is firm," the trader says. (natasha.brereton@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,409.10/oz, up $5.10 since the New York close on buying in Tokyo as the JPY weakens, and oil's rally after UN authorization of Western military action against Col. Moammar Gadhafi's forces in Libya. "Should oil prices move higher yet again, gold could be back in demand as a hedge against inflation," says Phillip Futures. Nymex April crude is at $103/bbl up $1.58, spurred by uncertainty over the timing and scale of the military moves in Libya. Tocom February 2012 gold is at Y3,700/gram, up Y139 as G7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  encourages investors back into asset markets, including gold.(james.campbell@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0232 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is holding fairly steady around 81.41 on EBS as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF's JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has set off strong JPY-buying flows from non-Japanese investors, say several dealers. "The MOF is still in the market, and that's why the dollar is being stabilized around this level because there are many non-Japanese investors who are buying the yen," one says. Still, the amount of total \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far is likely less than Y1 trillion, they say. "As (finance minister) Noda mentioned, we may see another big wave coming in European trading hours," another dealer adds; Noda earlier mentioned the possibility of the ECB joining the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaign. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] The G-7 joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb sharp JPY gains "is likely to have a meaningful impact in discouraging speculative JPY purchases" for now but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may remain under upward pressure so long as Japan's troubled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors, as well as geopolitical concerns, weigh on investors' willingness to buy higher-yielding, riskier \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "General risk aversion, currently driven by the unstable situation at Japan's damaged nuclear power plant and ongoing tension in the Middle East, tends to strengthen the JPY," Yamamoto says. "In order to ease the upward pressure on the JPY, a resolution of the nuclear plant's problems and an easing of tensions in the Middle East are required." He says the key level to watch on any further USD/JPY rises following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that started earlier is 83.30, the high hit after last Friday's earthquake. The USD/JPY is last at 81.44. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls after a lower central parity is set at 6.5668 from Thursday's 6.5690; the central parity is close to the record low of 6.5651 set Mar. 7. In OTC trade, the USD/CNY is at 6.5708 from 6.5744 late Thursday; a Beijing-based local bank trader tips trade in a 6.5680-6.5780 band. "The fixing was lower than expected. I don't think it's a signal that the PBOC wants to let the yuan appreciate, rather it's to manage the yuan's stability during the Japanese crisis. Starting yesterday, we're seeing a lot of export settlements; that has pushed the dollar higher against the yuan," she says. Offshore, the one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4470/6.4520 from 6.4600/6.4640 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.5% at 458.12, led by selling in select blue-chip stocks on concerns over the global impact of Japan's earthquake and the Libyan strife. Dealers say trading volume is thin because many institutional investors are staying on the sidelines amid rising global uncertainties which could result in disadvantages for the local economy; "one of the key concerns is a possible rise in oil prices, which could hurt our economic recovery," says a Hanoi-based brokerage trader. He adds the fact that foreigners turned net sellers in recent sessions is also hurting local sentiment. Among leading decliners, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bao Viet Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BVH.VH) is down 4.5% at VND74,500, \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroVietnam Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PVF.VH) is down 2.7% at VND21,300 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \f28 \uc2 \f2 is down 1.8% at VND44,700. The trader tips immediate support for the key index at 455. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Capital Economics has cut its growth forecast for Japan for the 2011 calendar year following last Friday's earthquake and tsunami. It now expects growth to be flat on-year compared with its earlier 1.0% growth forecast. "We were already expecting GDP to contract for a second successive quarter in the first three months of 2011 and now expect this recession to be extended at least until the middle of the year," it adds. But it raises its 2012 forecast to +1.75% from +1.0% on the likely boost in reconstruction spending. It notes while the destruction of homes, commercial buildings and infrastructure has no direct impact on GDP, which measures economic activity rather than changes in the stock of capital, the disruption to normal economic activity caused by the disaster will still have "a large and immediate adverse effect on GDP." It adds the impact of the power shutdowns across the country and weak sentiment among both businesses and consumers could also hit GDP.(puja.rajeev@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares rebound from a five-day, 3.6% retreat, spurred by overnight gains on Wall Street. The bellwether PSEi is up 0.8% at 3848.77 in moderate volume, with gainers beating decliners 73 to 18. Advancers are led by geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 2.3% to PHP6.31, SM Investments (SM.PH), up 0.9% to PHP515, and Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), up 1.4% to PHP11.72. "What we're seeing now is a technical rebound. After several days of losses, I guess some investors are thinking it is already a good time to buy. (But) there is still uncertainty because of what's happening in Japan," says Luz Lorenzo, market strategist at ATR-Kim Eng Securities. Traders peg resistance at 3900 and support at 3800. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is lower as local banks sell after G-7 agreed on joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the first time in a decade to weaken the yen, says senior trader at a local bank. He tips the pair to trade in a 7.7980-7.8030 band in the near term. "The G-7 agreement helps to calm the local and regional financial markets, while the rebound in local shares also lends support to the Hong Kong dollar," the trader says. The pair at 7.7998 from 7.8020 late Thursday. The HSI is up 4.2%. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds continue to rise amid easing concerns over further tightening measures, as an increasing amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ce