\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Asian CDS Tighten On G7 Pledge, Nuclear Relief\b0\par\par\uc2 1,745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0254 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian CDS are tighter as the market responds favorably to the G7's pledge to jointly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the JPY, while tentative signs of stabilization at Japan's quake-stricken nuclear reactors also provide support. "No news is good news, as so far we don't know what's going on," an Asia-based trader says, of the nuclear situation. The Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment-grade CDS index is at 109.5-110.5 bps vs Thursday's 115 bps. "It wouldn't be surprising if these tighter levels continued," so long as there isn't a serious deterioration in the Japan situation, he adds. Hyva Global's new $375 million 5-year global dollar bond deal, which was priced at par late Thursday to yield 8.625%, is trading around 102.375/102.625; suggesting that investors are "comfortable with the credit and pricing, and the market is firm," the trader says. (natasha.brereton@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,409.10/oz, up $5.10 since the New York close on buying in Tokyo as the JPY weakens, and oil's rally after UN authorization of Western military action against Col. Moammar Gadhafi's forces in Libya. "Should oil prices move higher yet again, gold could be back in demand as a hedge against inflation," says Phillip Futures. Nymex April crude is at $103/bbl up $1.58, spurred by uncertainty over the timing and scale of the military moves in Libya. Tocom February 2012 gold is at Y3,700/gram, up Y139 as G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  encourages investors back into asset markets, including gold.(james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0232 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is holding fairly steady around 81.41 on EBS as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set off strong JPY-buying flows from non-Japanese investors, say several dealers. "The MOF is still in the market, and that's why the dollar is being stabilized around this level because there are many non-Japanese investors who are buying the yen," one says. Still, the amount of total \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far is likely less than Y1 trillion, they say. "As (finance minister) Noda mentioned, we may see another big wave coming in European trading hours," another dealer adds; Noda earlier mentioned the possibility of the ECB joining the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] The G-7 joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb sharp JPY gains "is likely to have a meaningful impact in discouraging speculative JPY purchases" for now but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may remain under upward pressure so long as Japan's troubled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors, as well as geopolitical concerns, weigh on investors' willingness to buy higher-yielding, riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . "General risk aversion, currently driven by the unstable situation at Japan's damaged nuclear power plant and ongoing tension in the Middle East, tends to strengthen the JPY," Yamamoto says. "In order to ease the upward pressure on the JPY, a resolution of the nuclear plant's problems and an easing of tensions in the Middle East are required." He says the key level to watch on any further USD/JPY rises following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that started earlier is 83.30, the high hit after last Friday's earthquake. The USD/JPY is last at 81.44. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls after a lower central parity is set at 6.5668 from Thursday's 6.5690; the central parity is close to the record low of 6.5651 set Mar. 7. In OTC trade, the USD/CNY is at 6.5708 from 6.5744 late Thursday; a Beijing-based local bank trader tips trade in a 6.5680-6.5780 band. "The fixing was lower than expected. I don't think it's a signal that the PBOC wants to let the yuan appreciate, rather it's to manage the yuan's stability during the Japanese crisis. Starting yesterday, we're seeing a lot of export settlements; that has pushed the dollar higher against the yuan," she says. Offshore, the one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4470/6.4520 from 6.4600/6.4640 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.5% at 458.12, led by selling in select blue-chip stocks on concerns over the global impact of Japan's earthquake and the Libyan strife. Dealers say trading volume is thin because many institutional investors are staying on the sidelines amid rising global uncertainties which could result in disadvantages for the local economy; "one of the key concerns is a possible rise in oil prices, which could hurt our economic recovery," says a Hanoi-based brokerage trader. He adds the fact that foreigners turned net sellers in recent sessions is also hurting local sentiment. Among leading decliners, \uc2 Bao Viet Holdings\uc2  (BVH.VH) is down 4.5% at VND74,500, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) is down 2.7% at VND21,300 and \uc2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \uc2 is down 1.8% at VND44,700. The trader tips immediate support for the key index at 455. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Capital Economics has cut its growth forecast for Japan for the 2011 calendar year following last Friday's earthquake and tsunami. It now expects growth to be flat on-year compared with its earlier 1.0% growth forecast. "We were already expecting GDP to contract for a second successive quarter in the first three months of 2011 and now expect this recession to be extended at least until the middle of the year," it adds. But it raises its 2012 forecast to +1.75% from +1.0% on the likely boost in reconstruction spending. It notes while the destruction of homes, commercial buildings and infrastructure has no direct impact on GDP, which measures economic activity rather than changes in the stock of capital, the disruption to normal economic activity caused by the disaster will still have "a large and immediate adverse effect on GDP." It adds the impact of the power shutdowns across the country and weak sentiment among both businesses and consumers could also hit GDP.(puja.rajeev@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares rebound from a five-day, 3.6% retreat, spurred by overnight gains on Wall Street. The bellwether PSEi is up 0.8% at 3848.77 in moderate volume, with gainers beating decliners 73 to 18. Advancers are led by geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 2.3% to PHP6.31, SM Investments (SM.PH), up 0.9% to PHP515, and Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), up 1.4% to PHP11.72. "What we're seeing now is a technical rebound. After several days of losses, I guess some investors are thinking it is already a good time to buy. (But) there is still uncertainty because of what's happening in Japan," says Luz Lorenzo, market strategist at ATR-Kim Eng Securities. Traders peg resistance at 3900 and support at 3800. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is lower as local banks sell after G-7 agreed on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in a decade to weaken the yen, says senior trader at a local bank. He tips the pair to trade in a 7.7980-7.8030 band in the near term. "The G-7 agreement helps to calm the local and regional financial markets, while the rebound in local shares also lends support to the Hong Kong dollar," the trader says. The pair at 7.7998 from 7.8020 late Thursday. The HSI is up 4.2%. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds continue to rise amid easing concerns over further tightening measures, as an increasing amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill offerings suggests the PBOC doesn't need to hike banks' reserve requirement ratio to mop up excess liquidity, says a Shanghai-based trader with a local broker. The PBOC sold a combined CNY60 billion worth of bills in its regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, the largest weekly bill sale in more than four months. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond is at 127.23, up from Thursday's close of 127.22, and is expected in a 127.20-127.30 band for the day. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI stages a lackluster rebound and is up 0.3% at 22,343.03, as sentiment remains jittery. "Investors focus will still be on the nuclear leakage and the yen trend," says Ben Kwong, chief operating officer at \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 ; he tips the HSI's trading range today at 22,100-22,600. \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) rises 2.5% to HK$10.72 while Henderson Land (0012.HK) is up 1.2% at HK$46.15, after both reported better-than-expected results; China Mobile (0941.HK) rebounds 0.7% to HK$69.65 after a 4.2% post-results selloff in the past two days. Market volume is modest at HK$12.33 billion, suggesting bargain-hunting interest is not terribly strong. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are up 0.7% at 8341.43 on bargain-hunting after their recent losses, says \uc2 Polaris International Securities Investment Trust\uc2  analyst William Chen. He adds, Wall Street's rise overnight and rebounding Tokyo shares are also boosting the sentiment. However, "it's mostly small and medium-cap stocks that are rising sharply today, not the index heavyweights, so the benchmark index is unlikely to rise sharply." He tips the Taiex to find resistance at 8350-8400, while support is tipped at 8150. Tourism and construction sectors are strong; Formosa International Hotel (2707.TW) is up 5.4% at NT$442.00 and Ambassador Hotel (2704.TW) is up 3.9% at NT$41.05; Cathay Real Estate Development (2501.TW) is up 5.6% at NT$14.05 and \uc2 Kindom Construction\uc2  (2520.TW) is up 4.9% at NT$22.45. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2254ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
