] Taiwan shares are up 0.7% at 8341.43 on bargain-hunting after their recent losses, says \uc2 Polaris International Securities Investment Trust\uc2  analyst William Chen. He adds, Wall Street's rise overnight and rebounding Tokyo shares are also boosting the sentiment. However, "it's mostly small and medium-cap stocks that are rising sharply today, not the index heavyweights, so the benchmark index is unlikely to rise sharply." He tips the Taiex to find resistance at 8350-8400, while support is tipped at 8150. Tourism and construction sectors are strong; Formosa International Hotel (2707.TW) is up 5.4% at NT$442.00 and Ambassador Hotel (2704.TW) is up 3.9% at NT$41.05; Cathay Real Estate Development (2501.TW) is up 5.6% at NT$14.05 and \uc2 Kindom Construction\uc2  (2520.TW) is up 4.9% at NT$22.45. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD surges to 1.4079 from 1.4019 on EBS after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Noda mentions the possibility of ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say dealers. The pair may rise further on this talk, but any ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been confirmed yet, they say. "If the ECB is ever to step into the market, that will be during European trading hours, not Asian," one dealer says. EUR/JPY is at 114.47. With EUR/JPY also rising, it's unclear what the main driver for the EUR/USD gain is at present. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are likely to open higher, helped by gains across global markets and a stronger rupiah, traders say. They expect the main index to top 3520 after it ended down 1.3% Thursday at 3484.210. But, a trader with a foreign securities firm expects that "gains of the main index may be limited as retail investors will likely take cash ahead of the weekend." Bakrie Sumatra (UNSP.JK), and Sampoerna Agro (SGRO.JK) may lead gains after reporting strong 2010 earnings. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD is down at 29.540 vs 29.574 in late Asian trade Thursday on equity fund inflows, says a local bank trader. "But turnover is low this morning as investors don't want to build heavy positions before the weekend. Japan's nuclear crisis remains an overhang." He tips the pair to trade in a tight range of 29.495-29.570 for the session as Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely to prevent huge volatility. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] The JPY/KRW cross rate is down sharply at 13.8901 vs 14.3292 in late Seoul trade Thurday as stop-loss sell orders were triggered after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply against the greenback on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0000GMT, analysts say. They add, the Kospi's 0.5% rise is also lifting the won. "How aggressive (any) joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G-7 will be," may determine the JPY/KRW's near-term movement, says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji; she tips near-term support at 13.4000. But she adds, though the issue of political unrest in the Middle East and North Africa has somewhat shifted out of the market focus now, investors should keep an eye on issues in that region as well. The USD/KRW is at 1,129.50. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2224ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
