\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Capital Economics Cuts Japan 2011 GDP Growth Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 1,564 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Capital Economics has cut its growth forecast for Japan for the 2011 calendar year following last Friday's earthquake and tsunami. It now expects growth to be flat on-year compared with its earlier 1.0% growth forecast. "We were already expecting GDP to contract for a second successive quarter in the first three months of 2011 and now expect this recession to be extended at least until the middle of the year," it adds. But it raises its 2012 forecast to +1.75% from +1.0% on the likely boost in reconstruction spending. It notes while the destruction of homes, commercial buildings and infrastructure has no direct impact on GDP, which measures economic activity rather than changes in the stock of capital, the disruption to normal economic activity caused by the disaster will still have "a large and immediate adverse effect on GDP." It adds the impact of the power shutdowns across the country and weak sentiment among both businesses and consumers could also hit GDP.(puja.rajeev@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares rebound from a five-day, 3.6% retreat, spurred by overnight gains on Wall Street. The bellwether PSEi is up 0.8% at 3848.77 in moderate volume, with gainers beating decliners 73 to 18. Advancers are led by geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 2.3% to PHP6.31, SM Investments (SM.PH), up 0.9% to PHP515, and Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), up 1.4% to PHP11.72. "What we're seeing now is a technical rebound. After several days of losses, I guess some investors are thinking it is already a good time to buy. (But) there is still uncertainty because of what's happening in Japan," says Luz Lorenzo, market strategist at ATR-Kim Eng Securities. Traders peg resistance at 3900 and support at 3800. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is lower as local banks sell after G-7 agreed on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in a decade to weaken the yen, says senior trader at a local bank. He tips the pair to trade in a 7.7980-7.8030 band in the near term. "The G-7 agreement helps to calm the local and regional financial markets, while the rebound in local shares also lends support to the Hong Kong dollar," the trader says. The pair at 7.7998 from 7.8020 late Thursday. The HSI is up 4.2%. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds continue to rise amid easing concerns over further tightening measures, as an increasing amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill offerings suggests the PBOC doesn't need to hike banks' reserve requirement ratio to mop up excess liquidity, says a Shanghai-based trader with a local broker. The PBOC sold a combined CNY60 billion worth of bills in its regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, the largest weekly bill sale in more than four months. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond is at 127.23, up from Thursday's close of 127.22, and is expected in a 127.20-127.30 band for the day. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI stages a lackluster rebound and is up 0.3% at 22,343.03, as sentiment remains jittery. "Investors focus will still be on the nuclear leakage and the yen trend," says Ben Kwong, chief operating officer at \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 ; he tips the HSI's trading range today at 22,100-22,600. \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) rises 2.5% to HK$10.72 while Henderson Land (0012.HK) is up 1.2% at HK$46.15, after both reported bet