\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:China Govt Bonds Up; Concerns Over Tightening Wane\b0\par\par\uc2 1,521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds continue to rise amid easing concerns over further tightening measures, as an increasing amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bill offerings suggests the PBOC doesn't need to hike banks' reserve requirement ratio to mop up excess liquidity, says a Shanghai-based trader with a local broker. The PBOC sold a combined CNY60 billion worth of bills in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week, the largest weekly bill sale in more than four months. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond is at 127.23, up from Thursday's close of 127.22, and is expected in a 127.20-127.30 band for the day. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI stages a lackluster rebound and is up 0.3% at 22,343.03, as sentiment remains jittery. "Investors focus will still be on the nuclear leakage and the yen trend," says Ben Kwong, chief operating officer at \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 ; he tips the HSI's trading range today at 22,100-22,600. \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) rises 2.5% to HK$10.72 while Henderson Land (0012.HK) is up 1.2% at HK$46.15, after both reported better-than-expected results; China Mobile (0941.HK) rebounds 0.7% to HK$69.65 after a 4.2% post-results selloff in the past two days. Market volume is modest at HK$12.33 billion, suggesting bargain-hunting interest is not terribly strong. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are up 0.7% at 8341.43 on bargain-hunting after their recent losses, says \uc2 Polaris International Securities Investment Trust\uc2  analyst William Chen. He adds, Wall Street's rise overnight and rebounding Tokyo shares are also boosting the sentiment. However, "it's mostly small and medium-cap stocks that are rising sharply today, not the index heavyweights, so the benchmark index is unlikely to rise sharply." He tips the Taiex to find resistance at 8350-8400, while support is tipped at 8150. Tourism and construction sectors are strong; Formosa International Hotel (2707.TW) is up 5.4% at NT$442.00 and Ambassador Hotel (2704.TW) is up 3.9% at NT$41.05; Cathay Real Estate Development (2501.TW) is up 5.6% at NT$14.05 and \uc2 Kindom Construction\uc2  (2520.TW) is up 4.9% at NT$22.45. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD surges to 1.4079 from 1.4019 on EBS after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Noda mentions the possibility of ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say dealers. The pair may rise further on this talk, but any ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been confirmed yet, they say. "If the ECB is ever to step into the market, that will be during European trading hours, not Asian," one dealer says. EUR/JPY is at 114.47. With EUR/JPY also rising, it's unclear what the main driver for the EUR/USD gain is at present. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are likely to open higher, helped by gains across global markets and a stronger rupiah, traders say. They expect the main index to top 3520 after it ended down 1.3% Thursday at 3484.210. But, a trader with a foreign securities firm expects that "gains of the main index may be limited as retail investors will likely take cash ahead of the weekend." Bakrie Sumatra (UNSP.JK), and Sampoerna Agro (SGRO.JK) may lead gains after reporting strong 2010 earnings. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD is down at 29.540 vs 29.574 in late Asian trade Thursday on equity fund inflows, says a local bank trader. "But turnover is low this morning as investors don't want to build heavy positions before the weekend. Japan's nuclear crisis remains an overhang." He tips the pair to trade in a tight range of 29.495-29.570 for the session as Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely to prevent huge volatility. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] The JPY/KRW cross rate is down sharply at 13.8901 vs 14.3292 in late Seoul trade Thurday as stop-loss sell orders were triggered after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply against the greenback on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0000GMT, analysts say. They add, the Kospi's 0.5% rise is also lifting the won. "How aggressive (any) joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G-7 will be," may determine the JPY/KRW's near-term movement, says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji; she tips near-term support at 13.4000. But she adds, though the issue of political unrest in the Middle East and North Africa has somewhat shifted out of the market focus now, investors should keep an eye on issues in that region as well. The USD/KRW is at 1,129.50. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are expected to rise in tandem with healthy gains in most external bourses. "The SET Index should be able to stage a mild rebound after Thursday's fall. However, I don't expect it to go far as worries over Japan's nuclear issue and its impact still (present an) overhang," says BFIT Securities strategist Aphisit Limsupanark. Resistance is pegged at 1015, while support is at 996; shares closed down 0.6% at 1002.35 Thursday. Focus will be on developments at Japan's Fukushima nuclear power plant for trading cues. Heavyweight energy blue chips are expected to lead gains following the rise in global oil prices. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher, taking their cues from a stronger Nikkei, helped by the G-7 agreeing on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pare down yen strength. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 1.0% at 2927.23, and analysts peg immediate resistance at 2950. Still, "uncertainties over possible monetary tightening steps (in China) during the upcoming weekend is likely to cap the upside," says Capital Securities analyst Jacky Zhang. Coal miners are among leading gainers, rising on hopes of higher coal prices on the back of stronger oil prices; \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) adds 2.6% to CNY28.80 while Chine Coal Energy (601898.SH) rises 2.4% to CNY11.48. Brokerages advance on bargain buying after yesterday's declines, with Hong Yuan Securities (000562.SZ) up 2.0% at CNY17.34 and Citic Securities (600030.SH) 1.6% higher at CNY14.12. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.9% at 1296.11. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is lower at 8,776 vs 8,795 late Thursday as risk appetite improves after Japan's stocks rebound in early trade, dealers say. "But, it remains uncertain if the improvement in risk appetite will be sustained as fears over Japan's nuclear problems could resurface," a dealer says. Dealers tip a 8,765-8,805 range. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is up 0.5% at 2956.72, taking its cue from mostly higher regional markets after Wall Street's rise Thursday. Market volume is 204 million shares worth S$206 million with over five gainers per decliner on the broad market. Phillip Securities says "the intermediate term trend is still downward biased, though in the short term, price is neutral, consolidating between 2965 (resistance) and 2935/32 (support)." It adds, the index has overdone the downside and the STI is due for at least some consolidation. It tips resistance at 2965, then 2988 before 3000 and support at 2935/32, before a key support at 2915/2900. All eyes will be on \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG), which debuts this afternoon, after completing Southeast Asia's biggest ever IPO. \uc2 CapitaMalls Asia (JS8.SG) \uc2 is the only decliner on the STI so far, down 1.2% at S$1.67 after its strong gain Thursday. SIA (C6L.SG), \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG), Wilmar (34.SG), CityDev (C9.SG) and Olam (O32.SG) all rise over 1%. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are expected to move higher, taking their cue from overnight gains in Wall Street. The benchmark PSEi may head to 3850 after retreating 1.6% to close Thursday at 3817.38, a three-week low. "We're due for a rebound. We've been down for the last five days and I think investors are starting to shake off concerns over Japan," says Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Capital Market; the PSEi has retreated 3.6% in the last five days. Expected to lead advancers are blue chips PLDT (TEL.PH), \uc2 Aboitiz Equity Ventures\uc2  (AEV.PH) and Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH). Traders peg immediate support at 3800 and resistance at 3900. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2205ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
