hlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply against the greenback on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0000GMT, analysts say. They add, the Kospi's 0.5% rise is also lifting the won. "How aggressive (any) joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G-7 will be," may determine the JPY/KRW's near-term movement, says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji; she tips near-term support at 13.4000. But she adds, though the issue of political unrest in the Middle East and North Africa has somewhat shifted out of the market focus now, investors should keep an eye on issues in that region as well. The USD/KRW is at 1,129.50. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are expected to rise in tandem with healthy gains in most external bourses. "The SET Index should be able to stage a mild rebound after Thursday's fall. However, I don't expect it to go far as worries over Japan's nuclear issue and its impact still (present an) overhang," says BFIT Securities strategist Aphisit Limsupanark. Resistance is pegged at 1015, while support is at 996; shares closed down 0.6% at 1002.35 Thursday. Focus will be on developments at Japan's Fukushima nuclear power plant for trading cues. Heavyweight energy blue chips are expected to lead gains following the rise in global oil prices. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher, taking their cues from a stronger Nikkei, helped by the G-7 agreeing on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pare down yen strength. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 1.0% at 2927.23, and analysts peg immediate resistance at 2950. Still, "uncertainties over possible monetary tightening steps (in China) during the upcoming weekend is likely to cap the upside," says Capital Securities analyst Jacky Zhang. Coal miners are among leading gainers, rising on hopes of higher coal prices on the back of stronger oil prices; \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) adds 2.6% to CNY28.80 while Chine Coal Energy (601898.SH) rises 2.4% to CNY11.48. Brokerages advance on bargain buying after yesterday's declines, with Hong Yuan Securities (000562.SZ) up 2.0% at CNY17.34 and Citic Securities (600030.SH) 1.6% higher at CNY14.12. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.9% at 1296.11. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is lower at 8,776 vs 8,795 late Thursday as risk appetite improves after Japan's stocks rebound in early trade, dealers say. "But, it remains uncertain if the improvement in risk appetite will be sustained as fears over Japan's nuclear problems could resurface," a dealer says. Dealers tip a 8,765-8,805 range. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is up 0.5% at 2956.72, taking its cue from mostly higher regional markets after Wall Street's rise Thursday. Market volume is 204 million shares worth S$206 million with over five gainers per decliner on the broad market. Phillip Securities says "the intermediate term trend is still downward biased, though in the short term, price is neutral, consolidating between 2965 (resistance) and 2935/32 (support)." It adds, the index has overdone the downside and the STI is due for at least some consolidation. It tips resistance at 2965, then 2988 before 3000 and support at 2935/32, before a key support at 2915/2900. All eyes will be on \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG), which debuts this afternoon, after completing Southeast Asia's biggest ever IPO. \uc2 CapitaMalls Asia (JS8.SG) \uc2 is the only decliner on the STI so far, down 1.2% at S$1.67 after its strong gain Thursday. SIA (C6L.SG), \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG), Wilmar (34.SG), CityDev (C9.SG) and Olam (O32.SG) all rise over 1%. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are expected to move higher, taking their cue from overnight gains in Wall Street. The benchmark PSEi may head to 3850 after retreating 1.6% to close Thursday at 3817.38, a three-week low. "We're due for a rebound. We've been down for the last five days and I think investors are starting to shake off concerns over Japan," says Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Capital Market; the PSEi has retreated 3.6% in the last five days. Expected to lead advancers are blue chips PLDT (TEL.PH), \uc2 Aboitiz Equity Ventures\uc2  (AEV.PH) and Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH). Traders peg immediate support at 3800 and resistance at 3900. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] The \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's initial \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was probably small in size, possibly well less than Y600 billion, say Singapore dealers with direct knowledge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen is continuing, they say; "it's likely by the end of today we will have the amount exceeding Y2 trillion," one says. The USD/JPY is now at 81.23. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2203ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
