\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/TWD Down; 29.495-29.570 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD is down at 29.540 vs 29.574 in late Asian trade Thursday on equity fund inflows, says a local bank trader. "But turnover is low this morning as investors don't want to build heavy positions before the weekend. Japan's nuclear crisis remains an overhang." He tips the pair to trade in a tight range of 29.495-29.570 for the session as Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been monitoring the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market closely to prevent huge volatility. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] The JPY/KRW cross rate is down sharply at 13.8901 vs 14.3292 in late Seoul trade Thurday as stop-loss sell orders were triggered after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply against the greenback on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0000GMT, analysts say. They add, the Kospi's 0.5% rise is also lifting the won. "How aggressive (any) joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G-7 will be," may determine the JPY/KRW's near-term movement, says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji; she tips near-term support at 13.4000. But she adds, though the issue of political unrest in the Middle East and North Africa has somewhat shifted out of the market focus now, investors should keep an eye on issues in that region as well. The USD/KRW is at 1,129.50. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are expected to rise in tandem with healthy gains in most external bourses. "The SET Index should be able to stage a mild rebound after Thursday's fall. However, I don't expect it to go far as worries over Japan's nuclear issue and its impact still (present an) overhang," says BFIT Securities strategist Aphisit Limsupanark. Resistance is pegged at 1015, while support is at 996; shares closed down 0.6% at 1002.35 Thursday. Focus will be on developments at Japan's Fukushima nuclear power plant for trading cues. Heavyweight energy blue chips are expected to lead gains following the rise in global oil prices. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher, taking their cues from a stronger Nikkei, helped by the G-7 agreeing on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pare down yen strength. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 1.0% at 2927.23, and analysts peg immediate resistance at 2950. Still, "uncertainties over possible monetary tightening steps (in China) during the upcoming weekend is likely to cap the upside," says Capital Securities analyst Jacky Zhang. Coal miners are among leading gainers, rising on hopes of higher coal prices on the back of stronger oil prices; \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) adds 2.6% to CNY28.80 while Chine Coal Energy (601898.SH) rises 2.4% to CNY11.48. Brokerages advance on bargain buying after yesterday's declines, with Hong Yuan Securities (000562.SZ) up 2.0% at CNY17.34 and Citic Securities (600030.SH) 1.6% higher at CNY14.12. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.9% at 1296.11. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is lower at 8,776 vs 8,795 late Thursday as risk appetite improves after Japan's stocks rebound in early trade, dealers say. "But, it remains uncertain if the improvement in risk appetite will be sustained as fears over Japan's nuclear problems could resurface," a dealer says. Dealers tip a 8,765-8,805 range. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is up 0.5% at 2956.72, taking its cue from mostly higher regional markets after Wall Street's rise Thursday. Market volume is 204 million shares worth S$206 million with over five gainers per decliner on the broad market. Phillip Securities says "the intermediate term trend is still downward biased, though in the short term, price is neutral, consolidating between 2965 (resistance) and 2935/32 (support)." It adds, the index has overdone the downside and the STI is due for at least some consolidation. It tips resistance at 2965, then 2988 before 3000 and support at 2935/32, before a key support at 2915/2900. All eyes will be on \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG), which debuts this afternoon, after completing Southeast Asia's biggest ever IPO. \uc2 CapitaMalls Asia (JS8.SG) \uc2 is the only decliner on the STI so far, down 1.2% at S$1.67 after its strong gain Thursday. SIA (C6L.SG), \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG), Wilmar (34.SG), CityDev (C9.SG) and Olam (O32.SG) all rise over 1%. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are expected to move higher, taking their cue from overnight gains in Wall Street. The benchmark PSEi may head to 3850 after retreating 1.6% to close Thursday at 3817.38, a three-week low. "We're due for a rebound. We've been down for the last five days and I think investors are starting to shake off concerns over Japan," says Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Capital Market; the PSEi has retreated 3.6% in the last five days. Expected to lead advancers are blue chips PLDT (TEL.PH), \uc2 Aboitiz Equity Ventures\uc2  (AEV.PH) and Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH). Traders peg immediate support at 3800 and resistance at 3900. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] The \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's initial \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was probably small in size, possibly well less than Y600 billion, say Singapore dealers with direct knowledge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen is continuing, they say; "it's likely by the end of today we will have the amount exceeding Y2 trillion," one says. The USD/JPY is now at 81.23. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0134 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB edges lower to 30.30 from 30.32-30.33 late Thursday in Asia on selling mainly by exporters and some offshore banks. However, suspected mild buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  between 30.28 and 30.30 helps bring the pair off its 30.28 early low, say two dealers. "The dollar/baht has moved in a rather independent manner lately due mainly to continuous selling pressures from exporters. However, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's presence in the market, any near-term downside should be limited," says one dealer. Support for the day is pegged at 30.25 while resistance is at 30.35. All eyes are on developments at Japan's Fukushima nuclear power plant for potential near-term cues. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0134 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.1% at 1493.29, after opening slightly lower as concerns over Japan and the Middle East keep investors cautious. A local dealer says there is support from the strength in regional markets and strong U.S. stocks Thursday, but with oil prices rising, the recovery could be short-lived. Broader market breadth is positive, with some investors nibbling on bargains after recent declines: 250 gainers compared with 73 decliners. He tips resistance at 1500 and support at 1474. \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) is up 2.2% at MYR2.73, \uc2 British American Tobacco\uc2  (4162.KU) is up 0.7% at MYR45.32 and \uc2 MISC Bhd\uc2  (3816.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR7.58. Among losers, Public Bank (1295.KU) is down 1.1% at MYR16.72, \uc2 YTL Power\uc2  (6742.KU) is down 1.3% at MYR2.28 and RHB Capital (1066.KU) is down 0.6% at MYR7.96. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Even though past cases of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have marked turning points for the target \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's not yet clear that this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to keep the JPY from strengthening further ahead as the likely duration of joint efforts is unclear, says Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Citibank Japan; "at this moment I can't say whether today's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a turning point because this month is the final month for the Japanese fiscal year." Takashima adds "that was one reason why the government and the G-7 decided to step in, but next month we can't expect they will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so there is some possibility that the dollar could drop to around Y75 if they stop." But if any such renewed slides in USD/JPY should materialize, and drag the Nikkei down below 8,000, then it's likely that the G-7 could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, Takashima says. The USD/JPY is last at 81.26. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures fall, tracking losses in U.S. Treasurys overnight, while gains in the local stock market (the Nikkei is +2.7%) weigh; the contract is down 0.10 at 139.60 after hitting an intraday low of 139.30. Coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, which are supporting Tokyo shares, also weigh on JGBs. The benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield is up 1.5 bps at 1.215%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2153ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0001