\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI +0.5%; Due For Some Consolidation -Phillip\b0\par\par\uc2 1,566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is up 0.5% at 2956.72, taking its cue from mostly higher regional markets after Wall Street's rise Thursday. Market volume is 204 million shares worth S$206 million with over five gainers per decliner on the broad market. Phillip Securities says "the intermediate term trend is still downward biased, though in the short term, price is neutral, consolidating between 2965 (resistance) and 2935/32 (support)." It adds, the index has overdone the downside and the STI is due for at least some consolidation. It tips resistance at 2965, then 2988 before 3000 and support at 2935/32, before a key support at 2915/2900. All eyes will be on \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG), which debuts this afternoon, after completing Southeast Asia's biggest ever IPO. \uc2 CapitaMalls Asia (JS8.SG) \uc2 is the only decliner on the STI so far, down 1.2% at S$1.67 after its strong gain Thursday. SIA (C6L.SG), \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG), Wilmar (34.SG), CityDev (C9.SG) and Olam (O32.SG) all rise over 1%. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are expected to move higher, taking their cue from overnight gains in Wall Street. The benchmark PSEi may head to 3850 after retreating 1.6% to close Thursday at 3817.38, a three-week low. "We're due for a rebound. We've been down for the last five days and I think investors are starting to shake off concerns over Japan," says Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Capital Market; the PSEi has retreated 3.6% in the last five days. Expected to lead adva