roader market breadth is positive, with some investors nibbling on bargains after recent declines: 250 gainers compared with 73 decliners. He tips resistance at 1500 and support at 1474. \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) is up 2.2% at MYR2.73, \uc2 British American Tobacco\uc2  (4162.KU) is up 0.7% at MYR45.32 and \uc2 MISC Bhd\uc2  (3816.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR7.58. Among losers, Public Bank (1295.KU) is down 1.1% at MYR16.72, \uc2 YTL Power\uc2  (6742.KU) is down 1.3% at MYR2.28 and RHB Capital (1066.KU) is down 0.6% at MYR7.96. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Even though past cases of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have marked turning points for the target \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's not yet clear that this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to keep the JPY from strengthening further ahead as the likely duration of joint efforts is unclear, says Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Citibank Japan; "at this moment I can't say whether today's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a turning point because this month is the final month for the Japanese fiscal year." Takashima adds "that was one reason why the government and the G-7 decided to step in, but next month we can't expect they will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so there is some possibility that the dollar could drop to around Y75 if they stop." But if any such renewed slides in USD/JPY should materialize, and drag the Nikkei down below 8,000, then it's likely that the G-7 could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, Takashima says. The USD/JPY is last at 81.26. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures fall, tracking losses in U.S. Treasurys overnight, while gains in the local stock market (the Nikkei is +2.7%) weigh; the contract is down 0.10 at 139.60 after hitting an intraday low of 139.30. Coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, which are supporting Tokyo shares, also weigh on JGBs. The benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield is up 1.5 bps at 1.215%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds may fall, steepening the yield curve slightly, in line with U.S. Treasurys which eased overnight from a three-day rally, a trader with a local bank says. "We will see higher yields today. Some (primary dealers) are covering short, and are unloading to lighten their positions." She tips the two-year bond yield to gain 1-2 bps, and the 10-year yield to rise 2-3 bps. On Thursday, two-year yield slides 1 bp to 0.36%, while the 10-year yield ends flat at 2.30%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] The Japan MOF remains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but the size of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very likely to have been small, say five dealers based in Singapore, some of whom have seen direct flows going through the market related to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some of them say the campaign that kicked off at 0000 GMT was "Japan's solo act"; so far the JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being done only by Japan. Other central banks are expected to act later, during their market hours. The pair is at 81.26, off a post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  high of 81.49. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] Global agricultural markets may have posted a short-term bottom, according to commodity analysts at \uc2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group\uc2 , who noted a "decisive rebound" in farm product markets overnight after consolidating the day before. Wheat was the star performer with gains of 7%-8%, while rises for most other products were around 3% to 5%, they say, also looking at corn, soybeans, canola, sugar, cotton, coffee and cocoa. "The strength of the rebound in wheat overnight starts to give us some conviction that funds are already looking through the nuclear reactor problems in Japan and seeing this correction as a buying opportunity back into commodities." They add that the price action this time around in farm products already looks different from the last major "risk off" period around Feb. 22, which saw sell-offs for these products by 5%-7% in one session; "China could also be seeing value and buying on the dip." (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] Prospects for a swift resumption of Libyan oil exports near previous levels have receded further, after the UN passed a resolution authorizing a no-fly zone in the North African country, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Libya pumped 1.65 million barrels of crude a day prior to a popular uprising against the ruling regime of Col. Moammar Gadhafi, but currently two-thirds of production is offline. "While some countries, including some in Europe, might well still wish to make oil supply deals with the Libyan government, our expectation is that what will be a significantly reduced level of Libyan output is going to find it difficult to find markets to move into," BarCap says. Nymex crude for April delivery is now up $1.21 at $102.63 on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY may fall, tracking weakness in the USD index overnight. Friday's central parity will likely be set around 6.5680 from Thursday's 6.5690, says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. The pair is tipped to consolidate around 6.5700 vs 6.5744 in late Asian trade Thursday. Volume is thin these days because "the market expects the PBOC to stabilize the yuan until the dust settles in Japan, trading is quite thin," he says. (jean.yung@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2141ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110