\par\par \b \uc2 Japan, Backed By G-7, Lands Solid Blow To Yen; More Battles Await\b0\par\par\uc2 1,045 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Dollar rises more than Y2 as BOJ kicks of G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Upward pressure on yen likely to resume\par \par \uc2 --G-7 officials seen likely to defend the Y80.00 level\par \par \uc2 --Japan may want to weaken the yen further to aid struggling exporters\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Martin Vaughan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Japan's official yen-selling, backed by the Group of Seven industrial powers, knocked the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back Friday toward where it was before the massive earthquake a week ago. But while Tokyo landed a solid first punch, it faces many more rounds in its fight to keep the yen down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Finance Ministry, through the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , sold an estimated Y2 trillion in the Asia trading day, matching its one-day record, and with a promise that the rest of the G-7 central banks would join in the group's first concerted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since 2000. The group said it is fighting "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But market participants say upward pressure on the yen remains, despite Japan suffering its worst earthquake on record, a 10-meter tsunami and now a potential meltdown at a crippled nuclear power station. Speculators have been buying the yen on expectations that Japanese insurers and other companies will need to repatriate funds for payouts and rebuilding. And the turmoil has caused investors to exit positions that had sold yen to buy higher-yielding assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is "a battle in prospect for the authorities to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Mitul Kotecha, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy for Credit Agricole, wrote in a research note. "Round 1 has gone to the Japanese MOF and G-7, but there is still a long way to go, with prospects of huge repatriation flows likely to make the task of weakening the yen a difficult one."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's yen sales sent the dollar as high as Y82.00 from a low of Y79.19 around the time of the announcement. The dollar was at Y81.45 at 1009 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's not clear what would define success for the Finance Ministry, but there are several yardsticks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Senior Vice Finance Minister Fumihiko Igarashi said he wants to see the yen weaken to pre-quake levels, although this wasn't the ministry's official goal. Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said the Japanese authorities aren't targeting a specific level for the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Just before the 9.0-magnitude temblor struck northeastern Japan on March 11, the dollar popped to Y83.30 from Y82.81. Chris Walker, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London, said he expects the dollar to rise to about Y83.30 and not much further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That level is probably the most conceivable outcome," Walker said. "It's early days, but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a good chance of working. The way they have done this on a multilateral basis will make it much more likely to work."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is a risk that if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plows the dollars it buys from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into Treasurys, then U.S. yields will fall, narrowing the interest-rate spread over Japanese debt and putting more upward pressure on the yen, Walker said. Japan might shy away from buying too many T-bills for precisely that reason, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G-7 in their joint statement pledged to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets in the U.S., Europe, UK and Canada on Friday only. That doesn't foreclose the possibility of action next week or beyond. The ministry's Igarashi told Dow Jones Newswires he expects the G-7 to stay in the market next week if needed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Should the yen rebound, a key level to watch would be a dollar drop to the Y80.00 threshold, analysts and traders say. The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's precipitous slide in early Asia trade Thursday to a record low Y76.25 was one cause for the joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Finance Minister Noda said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Richard Jerram of Macquarie Securities said the G-7 has already largely achieved its goal by boosting the dollar back above Y80.00 and sending a clear signal it will defend that level. "You'd have to be a brave guy to buy the yen beyond 80 now," she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before September's one-day \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the last time \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a lone campaign over 15 months to March 2004, when it sold a total of Y35 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the full backing of the world's economic powers this time, that type of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't be necessary, said Jerram. "In 2003 and 2004, it took the markets quite a long time to realize Japan had virtually unlimited firepower" to keep the dollar over Y100, he said. "This time there is a clear event to justify it, and the reasons are blindingly obvious to everyone."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But will Japan be satisfied just to keep the yen in roughly the same range it was before the quake? The yen has been steadily appreciating since mid-2007, when the dollar peaked above Y124. A strong yen will make it that much more difficult for Japan's export-dependent economy to recover from the natural disasters and nuclear crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And a dollar at Y83.00 today may not be as palatable to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials as it was before the quake, said Masafumi Yamamoto, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclay's Capital. "Japan has less tolerance for a stronger yen than before," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese exporters would need the dollar to rise to Y86.30 just to break even, according to Credit Agricole, citing data from a recent Japanese government survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For this reason, some believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will not be just a one-off.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It will most likely not be enough to merely arrest the slide" in the dollar, given the severity of Japan's disasters, uncertainty over its crippled nuclear reactors and the dollar's recent collapse against the yen, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a research note. "Specific level or not, we believe officials will not rest until the trend has turned upward for the pair."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Martin Vaughan, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Takashi Nakamichi and Tatsuo Ito contributed to this article. [ 18-03-11 1020GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020110318e73i00024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichg