\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex May Trade In Range; Asian Markets Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA          11774.59  +161.29 +1.39% \par Nasdaq        2636.05   +19.23  +0.73% \par S&P 500       1273.72   +16.84  +1.34% \par FTSE 100      5696.11   +97.88  +1.75% \par Nikkei Stock  9120.93   +158.26 +1.77% \par Hang Seng     22371.61  +87.18  +0.39% \par Kospi         1975.78   +16.75  +0.86% \par SGX Nifty     5492.00   +13.00  +0.24% \par MONTH contract \par  \par USD/JPY       78.91-95     -1.15% \par Range         79.74-77.16 \par EUR/USD       1.4022-27    +0.93% \par Range         1.4052-1.3868 \par  \par CBOT Wheat May      $7.102 per bushel \par Comex Gold          $1,404.20    +$8.10 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,386.80-$1,405.40 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $101.42      +$3.44 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bombay Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 30-share Sensitive Index is likely to trade between 18,050 and 18,360 today.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The price action in the past few days has been devoid of any significant trend, and marked by a high degree of volatility. A shift out of the 18,050-18,360 band would set the tone for the next significant move.\par \par \uc2 Above 18,360, the index could rally to its major resistance at 18,450, while a fall below 18,050 could push the index to the major support at 17,920.\par \par \uc2 The National Stock Exchange's 50-share NSE Nifty could be confined to 5,420-5,480.\par \par \uc2 A move beyond 5,480 could push the index to major resistance at 5,520; while a fall below 5,420 could trigger a slide to major support at 5,370.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks Thursday closed out their first day of gains this week as rising oil prices boosted energy companies and efforts to tame Japan's nuclear crisis eased some fears.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After falling 3.6% in the week's first three sessions, the Dow Jones Industrial Average closed up 161.29 points, or 1.39%--its biggest point and percentage gain in two weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market's advances came as Japanese authorities claimed modest gains Thursday in their efforts to tame the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear-power complex by wetting it with some 100 tons of seawater using helicopters and fire trucks, an urgent effort to combat rising temperatures in two storage pools for spent nuclear fuel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. investors said the day's gains were unsurprising after the three-day slump.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Energy companies gained as crude-oil prices jumped more than 3.5%. Oil and gas exploration and production company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Noble Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumped 5.3%, while oilfield-services company Schlumberger rose 4.8% and drilling contractor Rowan gained 4.8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan led Asian stock markets higher Friday, as the Group of Seven industrialized nations agreed to a coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to staunch the yen's recent surge, providing some relief to Tokyo as the nation grapples with last week's crippling earthquake and an ongoing nuclear crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average, Australia's S&P/ASX 200, South Korea's Kospi Composite and New Zealand's NZX-50 were higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up 63 points in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo stocks rallied on the G-7 news, on relief that Japan's key exporting companies won't be hobbled by a rising yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen pulled back sharply against the U.S. dollar after the G-7 statement and as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. Japanese finance minister Yoshihiko Noda said each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its own timezones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese exporters such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Canon were up 2.2%-4.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Buying was spread across the market, with economically-sensitive stocks such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asahi Glass\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fast Retailing\f28 \uc2 \f2  advancing 2.4% and 6.0%, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some traders were circumspect about the Tokyo market's rise, noting that the coordinated G-7 action had been anticipated and Japanese officials still have a massive recovery and rebuilding task on hand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The problems at the nuclear plant are far from over, and we have a three-day weekend ahead, so it's hard to keep buying on the weaker yen alone," said Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities claimed modest gains Thursday in their efforts to tame a heavily damaged nuclear-power plant that has caused worries around the world.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar surged to Y81.11, from Y78.93 late Thursday in New York, and the euro was buying Y113.61, from Y110.58, and $1.3992 against the dollar, from $1.4020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen could lose more ground on the coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effort, said Richard Grace, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It looks like central banks will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as their market opens, which means round the clock \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and support," he said. "We'll probably see dollar/yen two or three big figures higher over the next 24 hours."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was at $1,407.20 per troy ounce, up $3.20 from its New York settlement Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April Nymex crude oil futures were up $1.39 at $102.81 per barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS March 18 \par 0930 IST  Mumbai:     Union renewable energy minister Farooq Abdullah at RuDiCON-2011 \par                       Rural Energy Security conference \par 0930 IST  Bangalore:  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Infosys\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO & managing director at CII annual members day event \par 1000 IST  Mumbai:     WNS CEO, Wipro BPO CEO and other BPO company executives at BPO summit \par 1000 IST  New Delhi:  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Suzlon Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief financial officer, Moser Baer chairman, \par                       \f28 \uc2 \f2 DCM Shriram Consolidated\f28 \uc2 \f2  senior managing director, ACC managing \par                       director, others at Green Manufacturing Summit \par 1100 IST  New Delhi:  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Union Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia India\f28 \uc2 \f2  managing director at joint \par                       press conference \par 1200 IST  Mumbai:     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boston Consulting Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman of Asia-Pacific at media roundtable \par                       discussion \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Dow Jones/WSJ \par 2ND UPDATE: G-7 PLEDGES RARE JOINT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS CRISIS-STRICKEN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SELLS YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group of Seven industrial powers pledged to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the first time in a decade as Japan dumped yen on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Friday to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's record surge from further damaging an economy ravaged by earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster. (published 0204 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BERNANKE, TRICHET FIRST AMONG G-7 CENTRAL BANKERS SAYING WOULD HELP IN YEN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to curb the value of the yen was unanimously backed by all members of the Group of Seven industrialized nations, with U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke and European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet the first in expressing their support for the coordinated move, a G-7 official told Dow Jones Newswires. (published 0206 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR SURGES AGAINST YEN AS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar surged against the yen after the Group of Seven finance ministers agreed to joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen, as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  struggles with multiple natural disasters and an ongoing nuclear emergency. (published 0110 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ SHIRAKAWA: BOJ TO CONTINUE TO PROVIDE AMPLE LIQUIDITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Friday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to pump cash into Japan's financial system as needed to stabilize domestic financial markets as Japan and its Group of Seven counterparts act jointly to stabilize \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates. (published 0107 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: EBA: EU STRESS TESTS WILL FOCUS ON BANKS' CORE TIER 1 CAPITAL LEVELS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European officials unveiled some details of the "stress tests" they plan to run on the continent's banks, hoping to show that the exercise will be tough enough to defuse fears about the sector's health. (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: BEHIND YEN'S RECORD SURGE; INDIVIDUAL INVESTORS, HEDGE FUNDS SCRAMBLED TO BUY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A day after a heart-stopping rally drove the Japanese yen to one of its sharpest ascents in history, traders and bankers blamed a freak onslaught of forced buying by Japanese individual investors and hedge funds--a barrage that came at the exact time of day when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is at its most vulnerable. (published 2326 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: BOEING SAYS FUTURE PART SHORTAGES POSSIBLE FROM JAPANESE SUPPLIERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While monitoring the ongoing crises in Japan, officials from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (BA) on Thursday said they are determining how to deal with possible airplane parts shortages from suppliers there. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RENAULT SAMSUNG TO CUT PRODUCTION ON POSSIBLE LACK OF PARTS FROM JAPAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Renault Samsung Motors Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Friday it will reduce production to prepare for a possible lack of auto parts coming from Japan in the wake of earthquakes, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 GM Korea Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . is considering cutting output for the same reason. (published 2347 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: SAMSUNG EXPECTS RECORD 2011 PROFIT DESPITE TOUGH CONDITIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . expects to return a record operating profit in 2011 even though trading conditions will remain difficult as intensifying competition among electronics companies and global economic headwinds will put pressure on margins. (published 0118 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par QANTAS SETTLES WITH NZ AUTHORITIES OVER PRICE FIXING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Qantas Airways Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Friday it has reached a settlement with New Zealand authorities in relation to price fixing conduct in its freight division. (published 2309 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TELSTRA DELAYS VOTE ON NATIONAL BROADBAND NETWORK DEAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Telstra Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., Australia's largest telecommunications provider, said Friday negotiations with state-owned NBN Co. over its part in the development of a A$36 billion national broadband network continue to progress but that it will have to delay a shareholder vote on the deal. (published 2313 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ANZ TARGETS ASIA, EUROPE, AMERICA TO GENERATE 25-30% OF PROFIT BY 2017 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 18-03-11 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said in a strategy update Friday it aims to generate 25-30% of profit from Asia, Europe and America by the end of 2017. (published 2355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Indian Media \par Three Indian Companies To Bid For \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bandanna Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  Coal Assets - Economic Times \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 18-03-11 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 62500   \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020110318e7