\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks up, oil off on Libya, yen sags on G7 move\b0\par\par\uc2 1,080 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 20:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Libya declares ceasefire, stocks rise\par \par \uc2 * G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen\par \par \uc2 * Dollar jumps more than 3 percent vs. yen\par \par \uc2 * Oil rally reversed after Libya move\par \par \uc2 (Updates to late afternoon)\par \par \uc2 Al Yoon\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 18 (Reuters) - Global stocks rose on Friday as traders took on riskier investments following a Libya ceasefire that reduced tension in the region, and after several central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the yen.\par \par \uc2 Trading capped a week of extreme volatility marked by Wall Street's fear gauge, the VIX, breaking through 31 for the first time since July. Stock market volumes surged on down days and fell on up days.\par \par \uc2 Oil fell from earlier highs after Libya declared a ceasefire in the country to protect civilians and comply with a United Nations resolution passed overnight. It had surged after the U.N. Security Council endorsed a no-fly zone for Libya, and authorized "all necessary measures" to protect civilians against Gaddafi's forces. [ID:nLDE72H00K]\par \par \uc2 "That (Mideast unrest) quieting down and Japan quieting down will lead to buying," said Stephen Massocca, managing director at Wedbush Morgan in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude <LCOc1> had jumped above $117 a barrel on worries of escalating unrest in oil-rich countries after the U.N. action to contain Libya's Muammar Gaddafi. Brent for May delivery shed 86 cents, or 0.8 percent, to $114.04 a barrel after the ceasefire. U.S. crude fell 35 cents to settle at $101.07 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed 2.6 percent to 80.86 yen, retreating from a session high of around 82 yen <JPY=>, following the G7 announcement to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp rise in recent days.\par \par \uc2 The show of solidarity by the G7 major developed economies to support Japan through its biggest crisis since World War Two comes a day after the yen soared to a record 76.25 per dollar in chaotic trading. It is the first coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the G7 in a decade.\par \par \uc2 The G7 "is just helping sentiment, and stocks sensitive to risk will push on. But optimism is going to be guarded as there are no firm resolutions surrounding the Japanese nuclear crisis and the Middle East, and anything can happen on the weekend," said Giles Watts, head of equities at City Index in London.\par \par \uc2 World shares as measured by MSCI <.MIWD00000PUS> advanced 0.6 percent. That gain helped the index erase some of its 5.6 percent drop over the past six trading days and brought the index near even for 2011.\par \par \uc2 WALL ST UP ON BANKS, NIKKEI'S REBOUND\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, stocks held gains but pulled back from session highs due to caution before a long weekend in Japan, where markets will be closed on Monday for a holiday.\par \par \uc2 Friday also marked the end of the two-day quadruple witching period. Quadruple witching is the expiration and settlement of March stock-index futures, single-stock futuers, equity options and stock-index options.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> gained 85.74 points, or 0.73 percent, to 11,860.33. The Standard & Poor's 500 Index <.SPX> added 5.38 points, or 0.42 percent, to 1,279.10. The Nasdaq Composite Index <.IXIC> rose 8.57 points, or 0.33 percent, to 2,644.62 -- well off its session high of 2,665.56.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, all three major U.S. stock indexes rose more than 1 percent to touch session highs. The Dow hit an intraday high at 11,927.09, while the S&P rose as high as 1,288.88.\par \par \uc2 <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS:\par \par \uc2 -- G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/sub68r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/sub68r}}}\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  disaster {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/fyh58r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/fyh58r}}}\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 Financial stocks rose after the Federal Reserve notified some of the largest U.S. banks that they passed a second round of stress tests. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would let 19 of those banks use some of their massive capital cushions to buy back shares, repay the government and boost dividends.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2  and \uc2 Wells Fargo & Co\uc2 . are among those planning dividend boosts. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's stock gained 2.4 percent to $45.64, while \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  shares added 1.3 percent to $31.76.\par \par \uc2 Industrial shares also rose on bets they could benefit in Japan's rebuilding effort. \uc2 General Electric Co\uc2  rose 0.5 percent to $19.32, while Caterpillar advanced 1.9 percent to $105.07.\par \par \uc2 Before the U.S. trading open, European equities pared earlier gains after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised lenders' required reserve ratios. The FTSEurofirst 300 <.FTEU3> added 0.2 percent to close at 1,088.82.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei share index <.N225> climbed 2.7 percent, recouping some of the week's losses as Japan reeled from the aftermath of an earthquake, tsunami and nuclear power plant crisis. [ID:nTOPNOW4]\par \par \uc2 YEN AND BONDS SLIP, GOLD GAINS\par \par \uc2 The euro <EURJPY=R> rose 3.5 percent to 114.56 yen, after climbing to a session high of 115.56 yen earlier. Some traders noted the scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was so far a tame effort to stem the yen's surge.\par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> rose to a four-month high against the dollar of about $1.4145 after the euro/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some market observers said even massive official selling might not restrain the yen for long, pointing to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September 2010 when it sold a huge 2.1 trillion yen, or around $25 billion worth, but only managed to push the dollar up to 85.77 yen from 82.85 yen.\par \par \uc2 "It would need to be concerted and aggressive ... and even then I'm skeptical," said Richard Wiltshire, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at ETX Capital in London.\par \par \uc2 A New York Federal Reserve spokesman said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had joined the G7 in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Demand for the safety of government debt eased.The price of the benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury note <US10YT=RR> dipped 5/32, nudging its yield up 0.02 percentage point to 3.28 percent.\par \par \uc2 Gold <XAU=> rose $15.00 to $1,417.40 an ounce, but was off a record around $1,444 hit last week.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Anirban Nag, Joanne Frearson and Chris Reese, Richard Leong, Edward Krudy, Chuck Mikolajczak, Steven C. Johnson and Emelia Sithole-Matarise; Writing by Al Yoon; Editing by Jan Paschal)\par \par \uc2 holdjlpMARKETS-GLOBAL (WRAPUP 7)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110321e73i001ec\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
