\par\par \b \uc2 Sterling firm vs Japanese yen, but lags against euro\b0\par\par\uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * UK pound firms vs yen after coordinated G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * But consumer confidence data, Bean's comments weigh\par \par \uc2 * Euro/sterling near 4-month low, hits high of 87.61 pence\par \par \uc2 By Niki O'Callaghan\par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 18 (Reuters) - Sterling was firmer on the yen on Friday, with the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under broad pressure after the Bank of England \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as part of the coordinated Group of Seven effort to stem its recent surge.\par \par \uc2 The British pound rose to 132.30 yen <GBPJPY=R> from around 131.60 just after 0800 GMT when the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started. But it gave up most of those gains to trade at 131.70, although it was still up more than 4 yen on the day, or over 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The last joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a decade ago when the G7 rich nations moved to turn a slumping euro following its 1999 launch.\par \par \uc2 Friday's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came after Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreed with central banks of the United States, Britain and Canada as well as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Traders estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of England (BOE) could be in the region of 400 million pounds.\par \par \uc2 "It was confirmed that the BoE had joined the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it is unknown into which exchange markets and how," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "More BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible and the BoE will show solidarity with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and any further coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Sterling also recouped some of its earlier losses against the dollar and the euro, having come under pressure on dovish comments from a Bank of England policymaker and data showing a drop in consumer confidence.\par \par \uc2 British consumer confidence fell in February to its lowest since records began in 2004, the \uc2 Nationwide Building Society\uc2 's survey showed, fuelling doubts about the strength of economic recovery and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to raise rates soon.\par \par \uc2 BoE Deputy Governor Charles Bean was also quoted as saying that Britain has entered into a "hazardous period" and inflation could be above target next year.\par \par \uc2 The pound <GBP=D4> was up 0.2 percent on the day at $1.6180 following a brief dip to $1.6059, with sell-stop orders tripped below $1.6100, traders said. Euro/sterling was up 0.6 percent at 87.41 <EURGBP=D4>, but off a high of 87.59 pence, its highest level since Nov. 4.\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts say sterling is likely to remain under pressure against the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors reprice the chances of a rate rise in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 While many expect the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates as early as next month, investors are pushing back the timing of the first hike in Britain.\par \par \uc2 "BoE's Bean's comments and the consumer data undermine market expectations of rate hikes," said Geraldine Concagh, economist at \uc2 AIB Group\uc2  Treasury.\par \par \uc2 "The economy does not look robust enough to start a tightening campaign. Markets have pushed back rate hike expectations and scaled back the magnitude of them."\par \par \uc2 Short-term interest rate markets are now pricing in a UK rate rise around September <ICAPSONIA> <BOEWATCH>, later than estimates earlier this month for a June rate increase.\par \par \uc2 "Right now, you'd want to be long euro/sterling. 88 pence is the figure to watch. Buy at 82, sell at 88." said Geoffrey Yu, G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-STERLING/MIDSESSION   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110321e73i000tv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
