\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China tightening marches on with 6th reserve rate rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,042 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * 3rd rise in banks reserve ratios this yr, 6th overall\par \par \uc2 * Many had expected tightening pause after Japan disaster\par \par \uc2 * Move will lock up about 350 bln yuan of bank deposits\par \par \uc2 * CPI at 4.9 percent y/y in Feb, to climb in coming months\par \par \uc2 * Increased reserve rate effective March 25\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 By Koh Gui Qing and Simon Rabinovitch\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, March 18 (Reuters) - China raised banks' required reserves again in a decision on Friday, the latest installment in its monetary tightening cycle that many had thought would be put on hold after Japan's devastating earthquake.\par \par \uc2 Its persistance with a campaign to rein in prices that has cooled hopes for the scale of world growth showed that the People's Bank of China views inflation, not an economic slowdown resulting from Japan's disaster, as the bigger risk.\par \par \uc2 China's announcement came just hours after the Group of Seven rich nations said they would jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm markets unnerved by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear crisis and to rein in the rising yen.\par \par \uc2 The 50-basis-point increase in required reserves was the third this year and lifts the mandatory ratio for the country's biggest banks to a record 20.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 The increase in reserves will lock up about 350 billion yuan ($53 billion) of cash that banks would otherwise have been able to lend in the world's second largest economy, making it an important tool for slowing money growth and dampening inflation.\par \par \uc2 "It's clear evidence that tightening is still on," said Stephen Green, an economist with Standard Chartered in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "An interest rate rise in these circumstances (following the earthquake) is difficult, but it looks like they're dealing with liquidity again," he said.\par \par \uc2 While markets have long expected more tightening by China, the timing of the move was surprising after several prominent economists predicted this week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would wait to gauge the impact of Japan's earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Japan bought billions of dollars in the market on Friday followed by other central banks, kicking off a rare round of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the G7 nations seeking to moderate the impact on markets of Japan's nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 A source told Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would leave the yen it sold during \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system rather than mopping it up, adding to the ample liquidity in the system -- which could run the risk of boosting inflation.\par \par \uc2 Juxtaposed against that, China's decision to mop up more funds gave a clear idea of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's priorities.\par \par \uc2 "This move ... is another sign that the tragic events in Japan are unlikely to have a significant impact on policy decisions elsewhere in Asia," said Brian Jackson, economist with \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "This decision clearly indicates that curbing price pressures is the key economic priority for Beijing right now."\par \par \uc2 European stocks and copper prices dipped after the reserve ratio increase, but market reaction was otherwise muted with many risky assets holding steady.\par \par \uc2 FOCUS ON INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Chinese consumer price inflation was unchanged from January at 4.9 percent in February and it is likely to tick up in the coming months because of a lower base of comparison.\par \par \uc2 China's top leaders have declared that their most important task this year is to control inflation. So far, complaints about rising prices have amounted to little more than grumbles, but serious inflation has sparked social unrest in China in the past.\par \par \uc2 To meet the official goal of keeping inflation to a 4 percent average this year, the government has raised interest rates three times and banks' reserve requirements six times since October, while also using a series of direct controls to cap price rises.\par \par \uc2 There are signs that the government has started to make progress. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday reported sharply lower bank lending growth last month, which will help reduce inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 "Companies are finding it difficult to get money from the banks. So I think there is a very aggressive monetary policy tightening in China. That's the main story, so inflation should start to come down," said Paul Cavey, economist with Macquarie Securities in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 In a short statement posted on its website, the People's Bank of China said the increase will be effective March 25.\par \par \uc2 FIGHTING INFLOWS\par \par \uc2 The required reserve increase announced on Friday was also seen as surprising against a background of apparently weaker capital inflows. Excess cash stemming from China's vast trade surplus has been a root cause of the country's run-up in prices, but it had a combined trade deficit of $890 million in the first two months of the year.\par \par \uc2 What's more, aggressive open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had led people to believe that it could afford to delay its next does of tightening.\par \par \uc2 "It is a fairly surprising move as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been quite successful in issuing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills in the last couple of days," said Xu Biao, an economist with \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  in Shenzhen.\par \par \uc2 "The liquidity stemming from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows in February may be stronger than expected and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to move more aggressively to soak up liquidity," he said.\par \par \uc2 Along with external sources of liquidity, there is about 750 billion yuan in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills that are set to mature by the end of April, putting extra pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drain excess cash from the economy.\par \par \uc2 It has conducted net drains in its open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the past two weeks, reviving them as it looks to use all tools at its disposal to dampen inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 But as the latest small step in a succession of tightening moves over the past half year, the required reserve increase will be taken in stride by domestic investors, said Qian Qimin, analyst with Shenyin and Wanguo Securities in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "The market has got used to required reserve ratio hikes, and there should be no big impact on share prices," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Zhou Xin, Jason Subler and Lu Jianxin; editing by Vidya Ranganathan and Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-ECONOMY/RRR (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110321e73i000ke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
