\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won, peso rebound as G7 helps BOJ on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; stay cautious\b0\par\par\uc2 994 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Won up 0.8 pct; stop-loss yen/won selling seen\par \par \uc2  * Japan nuclear crisis worries remain\par \par \uc2  * BOT spotted buying dlrs; talk of BSP dlr-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Updates with text, prices)\par \par \uc2  By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 18 (Reuters) - The South Korean won led a rebound in Asian emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday as an agreement by the Group of Seven to stem the yen's strength prompted investors to reduce dollar holdings, but investors were sceptical that regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could extend their gains.\par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan likely poured about 2 trillion yen ($25 billion) into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to push down the yen, Jiji news agency quoted a market sources as saying, after the G7 agreed to jointly and immediately \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surge to a record high and calm global markets.\par \par \uc2  The first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than a decade eased concerns about riskier assets which had battered markets earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2  The yen's sharp rise had threatened to add further pressure on the fragile Japanese economy after the country was hit by a violent earthquake and tsunami last week, triggering a crisis at a nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2  Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors remained cautious as workers struggled to cool several overheating reactors north of Tokyo.\par \par \uc2  "Everything depends on the nuclear situation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If that does not stabilise soon, then this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be for nothing," said Callum Henderson, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  Japanese engineers conceded that burying a crippled nuclear plant in sand and concrete may be the only way to prevent a catastrophic radiation release, the method used to seal huge leakages from Chernobyl in 1986. \par \par \uc2  While Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alleviated pressure on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it does not guarantee more demand for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts added.\par \par \uc2  "The move removed a bearish factor in short term but (does not provide) new growth momentum. Investors are likely to focus more on inflation again as global raw material prices may rise further on higher demand related to reconstructions in Japan," said Jeong My-young, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist of Samsung Futures in Seoul.\par \par \uc2  United Overseas Bank economist Ho Woei Chen in Singapore said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might even prompt other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars to check gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Reflecting that view, the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  was spotted buying dollars and there was talk of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Philippines' \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2  WON\par \par \uc2  The won  rose against the dollar as investors cleared dollar-long positions and on demand from exporters such as shipbuilders for settlements.\par \par \uc2  The South Korean unit also jumped more than 4.5 percent as dealers said some offshore players appeared to clear yen-long positions to stop losses.\par \par \uc2  But the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to strengthen past its previous high of 1,124.0 versus the greenback hit on Monday and Tuesday capped by dollar demand linked to domestic companies' dividend payments to foreign shareholders and importers' dollar bids.\par \par \uc2  "The mood was quite bullish for the won but the 1,125 seems to be a bottom (for dollar/won) as offshore players and importers are lined up to buy dollars around the level," said a foreign bank dealer in Seoul.\par \par \uc2  "We need to digest those dollar bids to see the won's further rise," he adds.\par \par \uc2  PESO\par \par \uc2  The peso   tracked other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with investors also covering dollar-short positions around 43.70 per dollar before the weekend.\par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was a talk of Bangko Sentral Pilipinas' dollar-buying to curb further strength in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the level. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar  strengthened, with investors gunning to test 1.2700 per dollar, a level which Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities had been spotted defending previously\par \par \uc2  Investors saw few factors that could push down the Singapore dollar yet, with markets unwilling as yet to challenge the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement.\par \par \uc2  But the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had cut some gains on real money flows around 1.2730 and as investors covered U.S. dollar-short positions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2