\par\par \b \uc2 JAPAN MARKETS-Shares jump, yen tumbles as G7 lends hand to  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,058 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrain yen, riskier assets gain\par \par \uc2 * Nikkei rises 2.7 pct on short-covering, weaker yen\par \par \uc2 * Shares down 10 percent on the week, $350 bln lost\par \par \uc2 * Dollar spikes more than 3 percent above 81 yen\par \par \uc2 * Too many uncertainties for clear market outlook\par \par \uc2 (Edits)\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano and Antoni Slodkowski\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 18 (Reuters) - Japanese shares jumped nearly 3 percent and the yen tumbled on Friday after the Group of Seven agreed on rare joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the soaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and calm markets jittery over Japan's nuclear power plant crisis.\par \par \uc2 The move by the G7 to support the country as Japan struggles to cope with its biggest crisis since World War Two comes a day after the yen soared to a record 76.25 in chaotic trading.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose more than 3 percent to around 81.75 yen after the G7 announcement, which came just as the Tokyo stock market opened.\par \par \uc2 "It was as clear as it could be and it was huge," said Imre Speizer, a senior strategist at Westpac Bank in Wellington. "They said they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the whole world will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is much bigger than earlier expected."\par \par \uc2 "This is huge and it's having commercial effect. There can be a lot more upside in dollar/yen before the next 24 hours is over. Global coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very rare, but when it occurs it is usually very successful."\par \par \uc2 Equity markets elsewhere in Asia rose after the G7 bid to stabilise global markets, with the MSCI ex-Japan index\par \par \uc2 up 1.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The G7 decision following a teleconference surprised markets. Investors had expected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in the rampant yen, but had not expected coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action by the world's wealthiest countries.\par \par \uc2 "This is the first coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  we have seen since 2000, so it's going to have a very huge resonating effect on the market," said Kathy Lien, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT in New York.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the Bank of Japan was immediately spotted buying dollars. Media reports and traders said it is likely to have bought more than $25 billion.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei share average rose 2.7 percent, but still closed down around 10 percent on the week, which wiped $350 billion off the stock market.\par \par \uc2 The drop marked its biggest weekly slide since the 2008 global financial crisis. Trading volume was closer to normal on Friday after the frantic trading earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond futures were little changed, while the spread on benchmark credit default swaps -- the cost of insuring against a sovereign default -- tightened.\par \par \uc2 Japanese stocks suffered their worst two-day slide on Monday and Tuesday since the 1987 global stock market crash. That followed a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and devastating tsunami on Friday that killed thousands and crippled the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power complex about 240 km (150 miles) north of Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Workers were struggling on Friday in the latest attempt to cool overheating reactors and prevent further radiation leaks.\par \par \uc2 LONG CORRECTION?\par \par \uc2 Shares of exporters, whose profits are eroded by a stronger yen, were prominent among the gainers on Friday as the G7 pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. \uc2 Fujifilm\uc2  rose 6.6 percent and electronic firm Kyocera gained 5.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 But traders said much of the gains were from short-covering, and the index may stall next week as investors wait to assess the full economic and human cost of the quake and efforts to prevent a catastrophic radiation leak from the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear complex.\par \par \uc2 "The main things investors are worried about now are the nuclear plant, the impact of the earthquake and tsunami on firms and power cuts putting pressure on Japanese manufacturers," said Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Investors will be waiting for earnings figures and forecasts in late April and early May for a better sense of how the triple disaster will affect individual companies.\par \par \uc2 "This correction will be very long," said Tetsuro Ii, CEO of Commons Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "We have the nuclear problems and impact of the quake on the economy, which is still hard to assess. I can't see the index bouncing back quickly to levels from before the quake."\par \par \uc2 "NERVOUS BATTLE"\par \par \uc2 The yen's surge had initially been prompted by expectations insurance companies and other firms would repatriate funds to meet the massive reconstruction costs of the disaster.\par \par \uc2 The move was exacerbated when a wave of stop-losses and options-related selling kicked in, propelling the yen past its previous record of 79.75 per dollar that occurred in the wake of the 1995 Kobe earthquake.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the yen's retreat beyond 81 yen on Friday would put pressure on investors to unwind long yen positions.\par \par \uc2 But others doubted the longer-term effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given the past experiences of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It looks like we'll see a nervous battle between the BOJ and the speculators," Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\par \par \uc2 "Hedge funds have expanded their asset holdings to unprecedented levels, so even if it's a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , effectively it may be similar to one-country \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So looking mid- long-term, I'm not sure if they'll be able to curb it."\par \par \uc2 Benchmark Japanese government 10-year bond futures fell early on, but recovered to close up 0.04 point to 139.74, while the 10-year yield on the cash bond edged up 1 basis point to 1.210 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The crisis is pouring cold water on the Japanese economy. Personal consumption will be weighed by concerns over electricity shortages," said Nobuto Yamazaki, executive fund manager at DIAM Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "Given such an environment, we can't expect a so-called 'good yield rise' in the near future. What would push up the yield of JGBs would be a worsening of Japan's fiscal positioning."\par \par \uc2 Benchmark five-year credit default swaps tightened around 10 basis points to about 110 basis points.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Masayuki Kitano in Singapore, Akiko Takeda in Tokyo and Wayne Cole and Ian Chua in Sydney; Writing by Alex Richardson; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-QUAKE/MARKETS  (UPDATE 6)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110321e73i00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
