\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Rise, Yen Falls As G-7 Agrees To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shri Navaratnam   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  led Asian stock markets higher Friday, as the Group of Seven industrialized nations agreed to a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to staunch the yen's recent surge, providing some relief to Tokyo as the nation grapples with last week's crippling earthquake and an ongoing nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average rose 2.5%, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 advanced 0.8%, South Korea's Kospi Composite was up 0.6% and New Zealand's NZX-50 tacked on 0.7%.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up 63 points in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rallied on the G-7 news, on relief that Japan's key exporting companies won't be hobbled by a rising yen as the nation struggles to cope with last week's devastating earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 The yen pulled back sharply against the U.S. dollar after the G-7 statement and as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Japanese finance minister Yoshihiko Noda said each \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its own timezones.\par \par \uc2 "In response to recent movements in the exchange rate of the yen associated with the tragic events in Japan, and at the request of the Japanese authorities, the authorities of the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada, and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will join with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on March 18, in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange markets," the G-7 statement said.\par \par \uc2 This was the first agreement on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since September 2000.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the outcome, traders have been anticipating some form of joint action after the dollar slumped to a new all-time low of Y76.25 early on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "What G-7 is telling us is that they will simply not tolerate a rapid strengthening in the yen," said Sean Callow, strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  bank. "Now in our view, the actual cost of the yen surge will not be significant. We estimate an economic loss of 3% of gross domestic product to Japan's economy from the earthquake and tsunami, but the impact of the subsequent yen rise should be much smaller," he said.\par \par \uc2 "This is more about preventing a one-sided speculative market than it is about the actual level of the yen in our view. And G-7 does not like one-sided speculative markets," Callow added.\par \par \uc2 The dollar surged to Y81.11, from Y78.93 late Thursday in New York, and the euro was buying Y113.61, from Y110.58, and $1.3992 against the dollar, from $1.4020.\par \par \uc2 The yen could lose more ground on the coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort, said Richard Grace, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 . "It looks like central banks will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their market opens, which means round the clock \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and support," he said. "We'll probably see dollar/yen two or three big figures higher over the next 24 hours."\par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters such as \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2 , \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2 , \uc2 Sony\uc2  and Canon were up 2.2%-4.2%.\par \par \uc2 Buying was spread across the market, with economically-sensitive stocks such as \uc2 Asahi Glass\uc2  and \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  also advancing 2.4% and 6.0% respectively.\par \par \uc2 Some traders were circumspect about the Tokyo market's rise, noting that the coordinated G-7 action had been anticipated and Japanese officials still have a massive recovery and rebuilding task on hand.\par \par \uc2 "The problems at the nuclear plant are far from over, and we have a three-day weekend ahead, so it's hard to keep buying on the weaker yen alone," said Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities claimed modest gains Thursday in their efforts to tame a heavily damaged nuclear-power plant that has caused worries around the world.\par \par \uc2 Markets in Australia, Korea and New Zealand were also buoyed by G-7 news.\par \par \uc2 In Sydney, energy stocks were stronger, aided by the rise in crude oil futures after the U.N. endorsed a no-fly zone in Libya and set the stage for a potential vote on a military move within a few hours.\par \par \uc2 April Nymex crude oil futures were up $1.39 at $102.81 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 Woodside Petroleum\uc2 , Santos and Oil Search were up 1.8%-3.0%. In Seoul, shipbuilders led gains on hopes for more orders with \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  up 0.7% and \uc2 Samsung Heavy Industries\uc2  up 1.3%.\par \par \uc2 Lead June Japanese government bond futures were down 0.13 at 139.57 points, weighed by the Nikkei's rise and the yen's sharp fall. The yield on the 10-year cash JGB was down two basis points at 1.22%\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,407.20 per troy ounce, up $3.20 from its New York settlement Thursday.\par \par \uc2 -Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4142; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 18-03-11 0124GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
