\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Bank Of Japan's Response To Crisis Unprecedented -HKMA\b0\par\par\uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 06:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds background on Bank of Japan comments in the third paragraph and with additional details on Hong Kong's involvement in the Chinese market beginning in the eighth paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 -HKMA Supports BOJ Massive Liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -HKMA Says BOJ Efforts Already Unprecedented\par \par \uc2 -Chinese Govt Supports HKMA Drive For Offshore Yuan Bond, \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Settlements Market\par \par \uc2 By Geoffrey Rogow\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of Japan's response to the many disasters now befalling that country has already been unprecedented, Norman Chan, chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ has done what it can," he said at a panel discussion in Sydney. "It's the duty of monetary authorities and central banks to ensure markets remain orderly."\par \par \uc2 Chan's comments come after the yen rose to a record high against the U.S. dollar in Asia trade earlier Thursday amid fears a nuclear crisis threatening the nation will get worse. Japanese insurance firms are expected to repatriate huge sums back into the country to meet the repair bill of the earthquake and tsunami that swamped the country nearly a week ago.\par \par \uc2 The yen has consolidated more recently, with markets looking for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to possibly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and as more dire overnight nuclear worries decline.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda has only said the MOF is closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, which he said are nervous on thin trade.\par \par \uc2 In response to the crises, the Bank of Japan announced the provision of emergency funds for a fourth straight day on Thursday, continuing its effort to calm concern about the impact of the earthquake and nuclear power crisis on the economy.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has offered a total of Y55.6 trillion of short-term funds in the past three days, including Y28 trillion in same-day loans.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Chan said China's government supports Hong Kong's drive to develop an offshore yuan bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements market. While Beijing still controls all cross-border flows from Hong Kong to China, he said the Chinese government is open to Hong Kong increasing its involvement.\par \par \uc2 "Hong Kong enjoys very strong policy support from the central Chinese government," he said.\par \par \uc2 Chan noted only 2.5% of all trade with China is done in yuan, forecasting that over time, he expects that percentage to reach 10% to 15%.\par \par \uc2 Still, the official said Hong Kong is unlikely to remain a monopoly on the offshore China bond market, with offshore bond issuance likely developing over time in other financial centers.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong has been the first foreign market allowed to issue Chinese debt, starting in 2009. In total, 16 issuers issued CNY36 billion in Dim Sum bonds last year out of Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Chan noted that the Hong Kong Dim Sum bond market has priced near the onshore market, limiting the amount of industry arbitrage between bonds issued in mainland China and those issued in Hong Kong. Overall, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has US$2 billion in Chinese bonds in its US$270 billion portfolio and has an allocation from the Chinese government to increase that amount to as much as US$15 billion.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been a growing part of Hong Kong's involvement with Chinese businesses, with Chan noting 70% of all yuan settlement occurred in Hong Kong in January.\par \par \uc2 -By Geoffrey Rogow, Dow Jones Newswires; +61-2-8272-4686; geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Enda Curran contributed to this article. [ 03-17-11 0102ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110317e73h0000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
