\par\par \b \uc2 AT A GLANCE: Water Cannon  \highlight22\uc2 Operations \highlight\uc2  Start After Long Delay\b0\par\par\uc2 2,086 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR PLANT STATUS:\par \par \uc2 Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant\par \par \uc2 After a long delay because of high radiation levels, water cannon cooling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  started Thursday evening at Japan's stricken nuclear plant.\par \par \uc2 The water cannon trucks were scheduled to shoot water at the reactor from a distance of 50 meters to try to cool the reactor's spent fuel pool, which is in danger of releasing higher levels of radiation if left uncooled.\par \par \uc2 Efforts during the day to link the stricken plant to the main electricity grid appeared to have been unsuccessful. If power can be restored to the reactor cooling functions, this could mark a turning point in efforts toward bringing the situation under control.\par \par \uc2 Authorities are concerned that if the spent fuel in Reactors No. 3 and No.4 is not cooled, large amounts of radiation may eventually be released. Since water was spotted in the spent fuel pool of Reactor No. 4 on Wednesday, Reactor No. 3 is seen as the first priority.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, several tons of water were dropped on Reactor No. 3 from SDF helicopters as a new approach was taken to the cooling \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Television pictures showed helicopters making repeated runs over the site with water spraying on top of the troubled reactor.\par \par \uc2 Evacuation area\par \par \uc2 Radius of 20 kilometers; those between 20 kilometers and 30 kilometers of the plant told to stay indoors.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 1\par \par \uc2 The first explosion at the plant happened at this reactor on Saturday, as hydrogen built up outside the containment vessel after the quake knocked out the cooling system. The top of the building housing the reactor was destroyed, but the containment vessel is believed to be intact. Low-level radioactive steam has been released to lower pressure in the vessel while seawater, despite being corrosive, is being used for cooling. The question is how much damage has been sustained to the fuel rods inside the reactor, which were temporarily exposed to air due to low water levels.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 2\par \par \uc2 Concern over suspected containment vessel damage. An explosion was heard Tuesday in the area around the suppression pool at the bottom of the vessel. Some high-level radiation is thought to have come from the reactor afterward in addition to low-level radioactive steam released to relieve pressure. The pool contains highly radioactive water, but while this part of the vessel was likely damaged, it is not known if this was enough to cause a leak. The fuel rods are also thought to have been damaged after hours of total exposure. Seawater cooling ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 3\par \par \uc2 High concern over spent fuel that could release high levels of radiation if not cooled. The used fuel in Reactor No. 3 is believed to be the most exposed and therefore to present the greatest danger.\par \par \uc2 Water cannon were being used to cool down the reactor Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, Self Defense Force helicopters poured water on the reactor from the air.\par \par \uc2 A hydrogen explosion similar to the blast at Reactor No. 1 blew off the top of the outer building on Sunday, but again the containment vessel is thought to have withstood the shock. Low-level radioactive steam has been released to relieve pressure. The fuel rods were temporarily exposed and are believed damaged.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 4\par \par \uc2 High concern over spent fuel that could release high levels of radiation if not cooled.\par \par \uc2 A Self Defense Force helicopter spotted water in the spent fuel pool Wednesday suggesting Reactor No. 3 was the greater priority.\par \par \uc2 Two fires have already struck this reactor, one on Tuesday, the second on Wednesday morning. The first fire left two eight-square-meter holes in the outer building. This reactor, like reactors Nos. 5 and 6, was undergoing a lengthy maintenance program when the quake struck and was less of an immediate concern than the active reactors. But spent fuel in 20 meter-deep pools of water near the top of the buildings housing these reactors must also be kept cool to avoid heating up and reacting. The water in the pool was thought to be possibly boiling on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 5\par \par \uc2 Japan's nuclear agency said the water levels were falling at the reactor. But the agency said it didn't expect any troubles in the near term at the reactor, which like No. 6, isn't located directly next to reactors Nos. 1-4, making it less susceptible to incidental damage from explosions. Although under maintenance, temperatures have risen slightly in the spent fuel pools.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 6\par \par \uc2 The Japan Nuclear Agency said it doesn't expect any troubles in the near-term also at Reactor No. 6. This reactor, located the furthest from Reactor No. 1, was also under maintenance when the quake hit. Temperatures have risen slightly in the spent fuel pools and are being carefully monitored.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires' coverage of the event and related news can be found by searching the code R/JA.\par \par \uc2 LATEST STORIES:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN METI: MASSIVE TOKYO BLACKOUT MAY BE AVOIDED UNDER CURRENT DEMAND LEVEL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's trade ministry said Thursday the greater Tokyo area--the nation's crucial economic center--will likely avert a major blackout for the rest of the day, as the use of electricity has fallen since the morning after the government called on the public to save power. (published 1033 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: JAPAN NUCLEAR EFFORTS HIT DELAYS AS US SOUNDS RADIATION ALARM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government renewed efforts to tame the nuclear power plant at the center of Japan's nuclear crisis after suffering some delays Thursday, even as it waited for results of its attempt to cool pools of hot nuclear waste on the scene with seawater carried by helicopter. (published 1138 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH NUCLEAR WATCHDOG: FUKUSHIMA SITUATION NOT STABILIZED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The French nuclear watchdog Autorite de Surete Nucleaire, or ASN, Thursday said the situation at the Japanese nuclear plant of Fukushima has not stabilized yet and it remains uncertain how it will evolve. (published 1051 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN NUCLEAR AGENCY: CONNECTING STRICKEN PLANT TO POWER GRID EXPECTED TO MAKE SITUATION MORE STABLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nuclear and Industrial Safety Agency on Thursday said once a power line is set up to connect the stricken Fukushima nuclear power plant to the national grid, all equipment will be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and engineers will have a better chance of getting smoldering reactors under control. (published 0208 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : YEN STAYS IN FOCUS, EURO SURGES ABOVE $1.40 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen inched higher against both the dollar and the euro in European trading hours Thursday as markets remained on high alert for any signs of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese authorities to weaken the yen. (published 1205 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 3RD UPDATE: JAPAN WARNS ON YEN'S RECORD SURGE; G-7 READIES TELECONFERENCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's spike to a record high against the dollar Thursday prompted warnings from Japanese officials, who blamed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength on speculation in an apparent attempt to justify potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0617 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TRADE MINISTER KAIEDA: VERY WORRIED ABOUT YEN RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's trade minister Thursday said he is "very concerned" about the soaring yen, suggesting a heightening sense of urgency within the government of Prime Minister Naoto Kan. (published 0714 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOKYO SHARES END DOWN 1.4%; FOCUS REMAINS ON NUCLEAR SITUATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo shares fell for the third time in four sessions on Thursday as investors remained nervous, even as the market pared losses in the afternoon on hopes Japanese officials were making progress in averting a full-blown nuclear disaster. (published 0729 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ OFFERS TO INJECT ANOTHER Y1 TLN SAME-DAY FUNDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan Thursday afternoon offered to inject an additional Y1 trillion of same-day funds into the short-term money market, continuing its effort to calm concern about the impact of the earthquake and ensuing nuclear power crisis on the economy. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ OFFERS ADDITIONAL Y5 TLN IMMEDIATE FUNDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan announced the provision of emergency funds for a fourth straight day on Thursday, continuing its effort to calm concern about the impact of the earthquake, tsunami and nuclear power crises on the economy. (published 0301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER NODA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOVES ARE NERVOUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Thursday he is closely watching \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, saying there have been nervous moves in thin trading conditions. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MOF NODA: WILL DISCUSS FINANCIAL MKTS IN G-7 TELECONFERENCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Thursday finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven nations will discuss Friday morning the impact of the massive earthquake on the Japanese economy and financial markets. (published 0432 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 3RD UPDATE: JAPAN REFINERY RUNS RECOVER; EXTERNAL SUPPLIES BEING LINED UP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's refining sector is gradually restoring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  following Friday's earthquake, but even by the end of the month refinery throughput is expected to remain well below pre-quake levels. (published 1108 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN'S YOSANO: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , STOCK MARKETS NOT IN TURMOIL - REUTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese markets are not so destabilized as to justify joint Group of Seven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or government purchases of shares, Reuters reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Economy Minister Kaoru Yosano as saying Thursday. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IAEA WARNED JAPAN OVER NUCLEAR QUAKE RISK, WIKILEAKS DOCUMENTS SHOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Atomic Energy Agency warned Japan two years ago that a strong earthquake could pose a "serious problem" for its nuclear power stations, the U.K. newspaper, The Daily Telegraph reported. (published 0150 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: JAPAN QUAKE TO HIT URANIUM SHIPMENTS IN SHORT-TERM - CANADA PREMIER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 17-03-11 1240GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada's Saskatchewan province, the world's largest exporter of uranium, expects overseas shipments of the mineral to take a hit following radiation concerns at nuclear reactors in Japan after Friday's earthquake and tsunami. (published 1110 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ FEB. MINUTES SHOW THE CALM BEFORE THE STORM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan policy board members said at the board's Feb. 14-15 meeting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would continue to support the economy by "pursuing powerful monetary easing," meeting minutes show--a pledge that came true a month later when a massive earthquake pushed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to move aggressively. (published 0416 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: MERKEL SIGNALS FASTER EXIT FROM NUCLEAR POWER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government will present a clear timetable for a change in energy policy away from nuclear power and into renewables, German Chancellor Angela Merkel said Thursday, signalling that Germany may speed up its exit from nuclear power. (published 1057 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MINISTER: COUNTRY'S INSURERS NOT SELLING OVERSEAS ASSETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the country's nuclear crisis that stemmed from Friday's massive earthquake appearing to get worse, traders pushed the dollar down to a record low against the yen in expectation that insurers would need extra funds currently invested overseas to pay claims. Japanese insurers are among the most cash-rich companies in the world and any indications of their asset allocation plans typically has a large impact on the market. (published 0217 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOKYO STOCK EXCHANGE WILL REMAIN OPEN IN CASE OF REGIONAL BLACKOUT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Tokyo Stock Exchange will remain open even if there is a blackout in the Tokyo region because the backup system is in place. (published 1151 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: BANK OF JAPAN'S RESPONSE TO CRISES UNPRECEDENTED -HKMA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's response to the many disasters now befalling that country has already been unprecedented, Norman Chan, chief executive of the Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on Thursday. (published 0447 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: OBAMA OFFERS JAPAN ANY SUPPORT NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. President Barack Obama on Thursday promised that his country will do "everything possible" to support Japan as it grapples with a nuclear crisis following a devastating earthquake and tsunami, the White House said in a news release. (published 0351 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US AUTHORIZES VOLUNTARY EVACUATION FOR FAMILIES OF AMERICAN GOVERNMENT PERSONNEL IN NORTHEAST JAPAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. State Department authorized late Wednesday the voluntary evacuation of the dependents of U.S. government personnel based in northeast Japan, as concerns mounted about the levels of radiation emanating from stricken Japanese nuclear reactors. (published 0426 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIANS URGED TO LEAVE TOKYO AMID NUCLEAR CRISIS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's government Thursday urged its citizens to consider leaving Tokyo as the situation at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant in Japan worsened and higher-than-normal radiation levels were detected about 240 kilometers away in the downtown area of the capital. (published 2352 GMT) [ 17-03-11 1240GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110317e73h0014m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
