\par\par \b \uc2 AT A GLANCE: Japan Quake: Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Plant Status\b0\par\par\uc2 2,017 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR PLANT STATUS:\par \par \uc2 Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant\par \par \uc2 In the latest attempt to cool down reactors at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, Japan's Self Defense Force dropped 7.5 tonnes of ocean water on top of the overheated units four times Thursday morning, while the nation's nuclear safety agency said that power cables from an outside source could be available by the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The overhead watering measure is seen as a highly unusual step and has never been tried before in Japan, but access to power may be a more significant development if the original cooling function can be successfully restored.\par \par \uc2 Authorities are concerned that if the spent fuel in Reactors No. 3 and No.4 is not cooled, large amounts of radiation may eventually be released. Since water was spotted in the spent fuel pool of Reactor No. 4 on Wednesday, Reactor No. 3 is seen as the first priority.\par \par \uc2 Television pictures showed helicopters making repeated runs over the site with water spraying on top of the troubled reactors. In addition, 11 water cannon trucks were expected to be used to spray additional water onto the units.\par \par \uc2 Evacuation area\par \par \uc2 Radius of 20 kilometers; those between 20 kilometers and 30 kilometers of the plant told to stay indoors.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 1\par \par \uc2 The first explosion at the plant happened at this reactor on Saturday, as hydrogen built up outside the containment vessel after the quake knocked out the cooling system. The top of the building housing the reactor was destroyed, but the containment vessel is believed to be intact. Low-level radioactive steam has been released to lower pressure in the vessel while seawater, despite being corrosive, is being used for cooling. The question is how much damage has been sustained to the fuel rods inside the reactor, which were temporarily exposed to air due to low water levels.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 2\par \par \uc2 Concern over suspected containment vessel damage. An explosion was heard Tuesday in the area around the suppression pool at the bottom of the vessel. Some high-level radiation is thought to have come from the reactor afterward in addition to low-level radioactive steam released to relieve pressure. The pool contains highly radioactive water, but while this part of the vessel was likely damaged, it is not known if this was enough to cause a leak. The fuel rods are also thought to have been damaged after hours of total exposure. Seawater cooling ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 3\par \par \uc2 High concern over spent fuel that could release high levels of radiation if not cooled. Self Defense Force helicopters started pouring water on the reactor from the air at 0084 GMT Thursday. The use of water cannon trucks was planned for later Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Government fears of possible damage to the containment vessel Wednesday were downplayed later in the day after white smoke was spotted coming from the reactor in the morning and radiation levels spiked.\par \par \uc2 An attempt to use helicopters for cooling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday was abandoned due to high radiation levels.\par \par \uc2 A hydrogen explosion similar to the blast at Reactor No. 1 blew off the top of the outer building on Sunday, but the containment vessel is thought to have withstood the shock. Low-level radioactive steam has been released to relieve pressure. The fuel rods were temporarily exposed and are believed damaged.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 4\par \par \uc2 High concern over spent fuel that could release high levels of radiation if not cooled.\par \par \uc2 A Self Defense Force helicopter spotted water in the spent fuel pool Wednesday suggesting Reactor No. 3 was the greater priority.\par \par \uc2 Two fires have already struck this reactor, one on Tuesday, the second on Wednesday morning. The first fire left two eight-square-meter holes in the outer building. This reactor, like reactors Nos. 5 and 6, was undergoing a lengthy maintenance program when the quake struck and was less of an immediate concern than the active reactors. But spent fuel in 20 meter-deep pools of water near the top of the buildings housing these reactors must also be kept cool to avoid heating up and reacting. The water in the pool was thought to be possibly boiling on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 5\par \par \uc2 Japan's nuclear agency said the water levels were falling at the reactor. But the agency said it didn't expect any troubles in the near term at the reactor, which like No. 6, isn't located directly next to reactors Nos. 1-4, making it less susceptible to incidental damage from explosions. Although under maintenance, temperatures have risen slightly in the spent fuel pools.\par \par \uc2 Reactor No. 6\par \par \uc2 The Japan Nuclear Agency said it doesn't expect any troubles in the near-term also at Reactor No. 6. This reactor, located the furthest from Reactor No. 1, was also under maintenance when the quake hit. Temperatures have risen slightly in the spent fuel pools and are being carefully monitored.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires' coverage of the event and related news can be found by searching the code R/JA.\par \par \uc2 LATEST STORIES:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HELICOPTER BEGINS DROPPING WATER ON STRICKEN POWER PLANT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the latest attempt to cool down reactors at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, Japan's Self Defense Force started dropping ocean water on top of the overheated units and said that power cables from an outside source would be available as early as this afternoon. (published 0117 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN NUCLEAR AGENCY: CONNECTING STRICKEN PLANT TO POWER GRID EXPECTED TO MAKE SITUATION MORE STABLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nuclear and Industrial Safety Agency on Thursday said once a power line is set up to connect the stricken Fukushima nuclear power plant to the national grid, all equipment will be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and engineers will have a better chance of getting smouldering reactors under control. (published 0208 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IAEA WARNED JAPAN OVER NUCLEAR QUAKE RISK, WIKILEAKS DOCUMENTS SHOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Atomic Energy Agency warned Japan two years ago that a strong earthquake could pose a "serious problem" for its nuclear power stations, the U.K. newspaper Daily Telegraph reported. (published 0150 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SPENT FUEL POOLS AT JAPAN PLANT 'BECOMING MORE SERIOUS,' US NUCLEAR CHIEF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. nuclear chief said Wednesday that a lack of water in a spent fuel pool at Japan's Fukushima nuclear power plant is "becoming more serious" and could cause an ignition, playing a role in the federal government's decision to tell U.S. citizens and military to stay at least 50 miles away from the site. (published 2352 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 MIZUHO BANK\f28 \uc2 \f2  ATMS BACK IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 In a big blow to Japan's third-largest bank, the core banking unit of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Financial Group Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., all of its 5,622 automatic teller machines throughout Japan and its Internet transaction system stopped functioning Thursday morning. The bank said normal functionality has since been regained after the roughly two-hour shutdown. (published 0319 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: TOKYO SHARES DOWN 2.1% BUT OFF LOWS; SLIGHT NUCLEAR RELIEF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo shares remained lower by the interval but the benchmark Nikkei 225 index had pared its early fall to be down 2.1%, supported by hopes of progress in bringing a key nuclear facility under control. (published 0332 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : YEN PULLS BACK FROM RECORD HIGH AS INVESTORS AWAIT POSSIBLE BOJ MOVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen pulled back from record highs reached against the dollar early Thursday in Asia, as markets braced for potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Japanese government to force the yen lower. (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN'S YOSANO: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , STOCK MARKETS NOT IN TURMOIL - REUTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese markets are not so destabilized as to justify joint Group of Seven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or government purchases of shares, Reuters reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Economy Minister Kaoru Yosano as saying Thursday. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: IN TOKYO, IT'S BUSINESS AS USUAL--FOR LOCALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most Japanese in Tokyo calmly went about their business Thursday, even as foreigners continued to leave the nation's capital following an abrupt reversal by the U.S., which instructed its troops and citizens to stay at least 50 miles away from the crippled reactors. (published 0230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MINISTER: COUNTRY'S INSURERS NOT SELLING OVERSEAS ASSETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the country's nuclear crisis that stemmed from Friday's massive earthquake appearing to get worse, traders pushed the dollar down to a record low against the yen in expectation that insurers would need extra funds currently invested overseas to pay claims. Japanese insurers are among the most cash-rich companies in the world and any indications of their asset allocation plans typically has a large impact on the market. (published 0217 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: TOKYO BOURSE: TRADE TO GO ON UNTIL EVACUATION ORDER REACHES CAPITAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Tokyo Stock Exchange said Thursday that trading will continue despite some calls from foreign brokerages to suspend trading amid growing fears over radiation levels at the earthquake-ravaged nuclear power facility in northeastern Japan. (published 0132 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER NODA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOVES ARE NERVOUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Thursday he is closely watching \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, saying there have been nervous moves in thin trading conditions. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN SHARES FALL AS JAPAN NUKE CONCERNS ESCALATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets dropped and the Japanese yen swung wildly after touching a record high against the U.S. dollar early Thursday as concerns about Japan's nuclear crisis escalated. (published 0104 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR COLLAPSES AGAINST THE YEN AS NUCLEAR WORRIES MOUNT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar collapsed to record lows against the yen in frenzied trade early in Asia's session Thursday as worries mount that Japan's nuclear crisis is spiralling out of control. (published 2306 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BANK OF JAPAN'S RESPONSE TO CRISIS UNPRECEDENTED -HKMA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 17-03-11 0400GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's response to the many disasters now befalling the country has been unprecedented, Norman Chan, Chief Executive of the Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on Thursday. (published 0335 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ OFFERS ADDITIONAL Y5 TLN IMMEDIATE FUNDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan announced the provision of emergency funds for a fourth straight day on Thursday, continuing its effort to calm concern about the impact of the earthquake and nuclear power crisis on the economy. (published 0301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: OBAMA OFFERS JAPAN ANY SUPPORT NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. President Barack Obama on Thursday promised that his country will do "everything possible" to support Japan as it grapples with a nuclear crisis following a devastating earthquake and tsunami, the White House said in a news release. (published 0351 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US DEPLOYS EXPERTS TO MONITOR JAPAN RADIOACTIVITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The United States will monitor radiation levels in Japan using its own equipment and experts, as workers grapple with the unfolding crisis at a quake-damaged nuclear power plant northeast of Tokyo. (published 0008 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIANS URGED TO LEAVE TOKYO AMID NUCLEAR CRISIS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's government Thursday urged its citizens to consider leaving Tokyo as the situation at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant in Japan worsened and higher-than-normal radiation levels were detected about 240 kilometers away in the downtown area of the capital. (published 2352 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/17) SPENT FUEL RODS AT PLANT POSE BIG RISK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's nuclear crisis now hangs largely on whether it can get control over the radioactive waste held in pools of water at the troubled Fukushima Daiichi power plant. (published 2348 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/17) US SOUNDS ALARM ON RADIATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fear about radiation dangers posed by Japan's nuclear crisis spiked as the U.S. instructed its troops and citizens to stay at least 50 miles away from the crippled reactors. (published 2346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/17)WORRIES RISE OVER JAPANESE SUPPLIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Worries about supply-chain disruptions from Japan's earthquake continued, with some Asian businesses saying they were slowing production to preserve stockpiles of crucial components. (published 2335 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/17) REACTORS ON FAULT LINES GETTING FRESH SCRUTINY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When Japan's Daiichi Fukushima nuclear power station was built, engineers and regulators knew it was in an area prone to earthquakes. But they didn't count on one this big. (published 2348 GMT) [ 17-03-11 0400GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110317e73h0009b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
