\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY,EUR/USD Steady Despite Libya; Japan Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 2,219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2314 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY and EUR/USD "are quite stable and a lot of that reflects the fact that no news is good news from Japan and we haven't seen a deterioration or change in what we are hearing from that part of the world," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Head of Trading Mike Hollows. He says the U.N. vote authorizing military force against Libya poses some event risk and "if anything I think people will be reducing risk today." He says market focused on the ongoing G-7 meeting but that's unlikely to shake things up too much; "There will certainly be a unified voice but I don't know if that is going to necessarily change the fundamental issues and clearly stabilizing these reactors is everything right now." The USD/JPY is at 79.17. He tips resistance to hold at 79.75 with support at 78.30. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Verbal support for USD/JPY from the G-7 is likely, and there is a case to be made for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in a report. It says with risk aversion spreading from Japan, and with market dynamics, rather than fundamentals, the most likely culprit, joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems like a low-risk way to help \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  turn sentiment. It adds, on the other hand, few would argue that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are fundamentally misaligned, requiring a long commitment of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and also 