\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Asia Outlook:Stocks Could Rise; \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Mkt Eyes G-7,Libya\b0\par\par\uc2 2,034 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2250 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets could move higher, bouncing from their recent losses and taking their cue from Thursday's gains on Wall Street; still, markets remain vulnerable to any negative headlines from Japan, as the country struggles with its nuclear crisis. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, investors are watching for the outcome of an ongoing meeting of G-7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors for indications about Japan's intentions in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets; a person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Dow Jones that Japanese authorities were "prepared for battle" against emboldened traders determined to buy yen. The USD/JPY is at 79.19, from 78.93 in late NY trade Thursday, the EUR/JPY is at 110.96, from 110.58 and the EUR/USD is at 1.4010, from 1.4020. The EUR/USD slips on news Egypt's military has begun shipping arms over the border to Libyan rebels with Washington's knowledge, according to U.S. and Libyan rebel officials, and news that the U.N. endorsed a no-fly zone over Libya. Not much on the data calendar, with Japan's revisions of January's indexes of business conditions at 0500 GMT and its monthly economic report. Elsewhere there's U.K.'s Nationwide consumer confidence index and the E.U.'s balance of payments and trade balance. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2246 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs are likely to open lower, following weakness in U.S. Treasurys, says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano; while overall market liquidity is expected to remain low, "movements in futures could be big, depending on how much stocks rebound," he adds. Sano expects the yield curve to flatten. Market payers are also focused on whether G-7 nations are willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coordinated fashion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the yen; G-7 finance ministers and central bankers are having a teleconference now. Lead June JGB futures are tipped in a 139.08-139.69 band vs the 139.70 last close, while the 10-year yield is eyed in a 1.205%-1.245% range vs 1.200%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2223 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is trading in the same $5 band it has held since the PM London gold fix Thursday shortly after the start of trade in Asia. The day's sharp lurch down as low as $1,386/oz, amid a surge in the yen as investors repatriated money and funds hit stop-loss orders on carry trades, was largely effaced over the course of the day, with an informal close at 2200 GMT at $1,402.79/oz a little above the previous day's $1,399.35/oz. Still, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  argues that the metal is showing a similar pattern to that seen during the financial crisis, when it hit a recent low of $681.75/oz. "Calculated in euros, the yellow metal is still hovering around the EUR1,000/oz mark. This means gold is not serving as a safe haven...investors currently appear to be selling gold to raise cash and cover margin calls." Spot gold was $1,403.70/oz at 2220 GMT, pushing at the upper end of the $1,399.50/oz-$1,404.95/oz band held since the PM gold fix at 1530 GMT. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2221 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads are bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.4% at 4575.0. Wall Street's S&P 500 rose 1.3% to 1273.72 on stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data, some hope of stabilization in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear crisis, and expectations of G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, with markets awaiting a G7 finance minister's teleconference from 2200 GMT. U.S. weekly jobless claims fell more than expected and the Philadelphia Fed's manufacturing survey beat expectations. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  rose 5.0% on stronger-than-expected third quarter revenue and fiscal 2011 earnings guidance. S&P 500 Financials rose 1.4% and Materials rose 1.7%, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs rising to equivalent A$44.58 vs A$43.70 locally. Energy rose 3.1%, with Nymex April crude oil up 3.5% to US$101.42 as Japan claimed progress in controlling the nuclear crisis, while Western countries appeared closer to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya. LME base metals rose, with copper and nickel up more than 3.0%. Spot gold rose to US$1,403.95 vs US$1,395.60 this time Thursday. S&P/ASX 200 last 4555.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2219 GMT [Dow Jones] The oil market is starting to refocus on tensions in the Middle East and North Africa, with the addition of Saudi Arabia to the geopolitical mix upping the need to raise the risk premium baked into crude futures. Saudi troops have been deployed in Bahrain to help contain protests, creating a potential powderkeg as other nearby states like Iran also vie for influence. Nymex crude for April delivery ended +$3.44 or 3.5% at $101.42 a barrel, settling above $100 for the first time since the start of the week. Saudis have played a critical role in covering the loss of more than 1 million barrels a day of Libyan supply, so any sign protests are intensifying in the Gulf increases market volatility. "Short of a renewed sharp downturn in global equities in conjunction with any new nuclear developments out of Japan, we feel nearby WTI futures could maintain the bulk of Thursday's gains with prices gravitating around the $100 mark into next week," says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. April Nymex crude is now up 53 cents at $101.95/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2201 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: The JPY/USD fell from a record high as demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Japan's nuclear crisis slipped on fears officials may sell yen to curb its strength. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen more than 8% over the past week on speculation Japanese institutions were selling foreign assets in order to send money back to the disaster-stricken country. Analysts, while most analysts acknowledge that repatriation is supporting the yen, they say widespread risk aversion has lead some investors to unwind "carry trades" that use the low-yielding yen to fund high-return, riskier positions elsewhere. "There will be some repatriation but this sort of trade is overblown and ridiculous," says Jessica Hoversen at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  in Chicago. "If [\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear crisis] stabilizes and we see coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollar/yen will stabilize and start to move higher," she says, adding that Japan's woeful economic fundamentals bode for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Late Thursday, USD/JPY was at 78.93 vs 79.60 late Wednesday, while EUR/JPY was at 110.58 vs 110.66, EUR/USD 1.4020 vs 1.3902, GBP/USD 1.6137 vs 1.6017, USD/CHF 0.8987 vs 0.9081 and the USD Index 75.968 from 76.681. Stocks gained as rising oil prices boosted energy companies and efforts to deal with radiation from Japan's damaged nuclear plant eased some fears over the crisis. Oil-and-gas \uc2 Noble Energy\uc2  jumped 5.3%, while oilfield-services company Schlumberger rose 4.8% and drilling contractor Rowan gained 4.8%. The Dow added 1.4% and the Nasdaq added 0.7%. Treasury bonds snapped a three-day rally as fears over Japan's nuclear crisis eased; 2-year yields rose 2.0 bps to 0.581%, 10-years up 4.0 bps at 3.244%. April Comex gold settled up $8.10 at $1,404.20/oz. Nymex April crude settled up $3.44 at $101.42/bbl as Japan claimed progress in controlling the crisis at the nuclear plant and Western countries appeared closer to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2119 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices could gain ground Friday after U.S. wheat futures finished sharply higher overnight, a rebound fueled by growing optimism about Japan's recovery. "As long as things aren't getting worse, that's a good sign," says Dax Wedemeyer, analyst at US Commodities. CBOT May surged 48.25 cents to US$7.1025/bushel. AUD/USD last 0.9803, holding below parity after falling from 1.0110 Tuesday. On Thursday, the most active ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January next year in New South Wales rose A$4.00 to A$284.00/metric ton, while those in Western Australia rose A$2.00 to A$294.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2114 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD continues to be supported by a pick-up in interest rate expectations relative to the USD, says BNZ strategist Kymberly Martin. She notes, however, the main driver of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to be risk appetite, with all eyes on Japan's nuclear-power crisis and ongoing worries about the Middle East. "If a risk event erupts in Japan or the Middle East then the U.S. will likely benefit on a relative basis from 'safe haven' flows," she says. The EUR/USD is at 1.4022. Martin puts support at 1.3855 and resistance at 1.4080. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2054 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand shares may open slightly higher "but we won't be skyrocketing" due to continuing volatility and worries about the ongoing nuclear crisis in Japan, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Forsyth Barr\f28 \uc2 \f2  broker David Price. He says index changes in Australia may continue to affect Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) and Telecom (TEL.NZ), although the fact that Fletcher Building's exposure to the S&P/ASX Indices will increase while Telecom's will be reduced has been largely priced in. He says trading will likely remain light as most investors will be sidelined by the immense volatility and all eyes remain firmly fixed on events in Japan. New Zealand shares ended +0.1% at 3329.587 Thursday. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 1850ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4:34 (Dow Jones) Long-term Tsy futures prices settle lower, partially retreating from Wednesday's massive flight to safety gains. Traders weren't as worried about Japan's nuclear crisis, and they received some better U.S. economic data. Jun 10-year Tsy notes dropped to morning low of 120-17. Settled at 120-30, down 10/32, near 3.25% cash yield equivalent. Jun classic Tsy bonds settled 18/32 lower at 121-30, off the 121-14 session low. Jun ultra Tsy bonds settled at 125-01, down 24/32. Classic Tsy bonds cover 15 to 25-yr maturities. Ultras are for bonds with maturities 25 years and up. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2354 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk blog at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 4:15 (Dow Jones) With oil and equity markets posting strong gains, the Canadian dollar climbs against its U.S. counterpart. Despite the relief rally, the markets are still susceptible to a two-way flows amid uncertainty about still-evolving nuclear crisis in Japan and the ongoing political turmoil in the Middle East and North Africa. The U.S. dollar was at C$0.9862, from C$0.9909 late Wednesday, according to data provider CQG. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2354 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk blog at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\uc2 \f2  [ 03-17-11 1850ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h000dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
